Cumulative incidence of coronavirus (COVID-19) which is increasing rapidly in the European Union/Greater Economic and United Kingdom, January 1 to March 15, 2020
The cumulative incidence of coronaviral (COVID-19) cases indicate a similar trend in the EU/European Economic and United Kingdom countries that confirm that, although the stages vary depending on the country, the COVID-19 pandemic is growing very rapidly in all countries.
Based on the experience of Italy, countries, hospitals, and intensive care units should increase its readiness for a surge of COVID-19 patients who will need health care, especially intensive care.
As of December 31, 2019, clusters of cases of physic cases with unidentified ethology were reported in Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
On January 9, 2020, China's Centers for Disease Control and Prevention reported the causative agent of this disease is a new coronavirus now the coronavirus for severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
Since that time, the disease caused by SARS-CoV-2 infection is called coronavirus disease (COVID-19).
Evidence until now shows that about 80% of COVID-19 sufferers experience a mild disease, namely airway infections with or without pneumonia, with most of these sufferers cured.
In about 14% of cases, COVID-19 develops into a more severe disease and requires hospital treatment, while 6% of other cases experience critical illnesses that require intensive care.
Mortality of patients being hospitalized due to COVID-19 is about 4%.
In this study, we reviewed the cumulative incidents of COVID-19 of the European Union (EU/EEA) and the United Kingdom (UK) then compared it to incidents in Hubei Province, China.
We also compare the current number of COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the U.K with the number of cases in Italy over January–15 March 31, 2020.
COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA and UK
After China, COVID-19 is experiencing further geographic disseminations and COVID-19 pandemic dynamic in other parts of the world is currently following pandemic dynamics in China.
On March 11, 2020, the Director General of the World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 a pandemic.
In Eurosurveillance 2020 published March 5, Spiteri et al. reported the first European with confirmed COVID-19 cases based on WHO's case definition.
At EU/EEA, three first confirmed cases were reported by France on January 24, 2020 on people returning from Wuhan, Hubei Province in China.
As of March 15, 2020, COVID-19 cases have been detected in all 30 EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom (UK), between December 31, 2019 and March 15, 2020 with that date included, there were 39,768 cases and 1,727 deaths were reported, with 17,750 cases and 1,441 deaths from Italy alone.
Getting cumulative figures and cumulative incidents of COVID-19 cases
At the European Center for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC), the number of COVID-19 cases notified by every country around the world is updated daily at 8.00. This amount is obtained only from official sources such as the Ministry of Health of the country, national and regional health authorities, and WHO.
This data is used to assess COVID-19 trends in EU/EEA and the UK, as well as compare it to trends in Italy.
As an active prevalence of COVID-19 cases, we are calculating the cumulative incidence of 14 days, so taking into account the natural journey of COVID-19, in each EU/EEA and UK countries, in the period January 1–March 15, 2020.
We also present the cumulative number of cases notified by each country until March 15, 2020 at 8.00 pm compared to Italian cases during the period January–15March 2020.
COVID-19 trends in EU/EEA and UK
Cumulative incident trends beheaded 14 days of COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the UK generally follow trends in Hubei Province (China) (Picture 1).
For EU/EEA and the UK as a whole, the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 began to increase around February 21, then increased sharply around February 28, 2020 (Auxiliary ingredients).
This trend is mainly driven by a rapid increase in the number of reported cases from Italy, but all other EU/EEA countries and the U.S. indicate a trend of increasing cumulative incidents of similar COVID-19 (additional Ingredients).
Figure 2 shows the cumulative number of COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the U.S
The data highlights that, until March 15 at 8:00 a.m 7, 15 other EU/EEA countries and the UK have reported the total number of cases comparable to the total number of Italian cases three weeks in advance.
Our results indicate that the number of COVID-19 cases that are notified is increasing rapidly in EU/EEA and the UK.
An eye-obscribed trend on the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 hints that the pandemic increased at comparable speeds in all countries.
This is in spite of the differences in the stages experienced by countries, variations of public health responses, and the possibility of differences in case definitions between countries and protocol differences to select patients who must be tested for COVID-19 confirmation, which includes follow-and-up testing.
At the beginning of March of 2020, doctors in the affected Italian region explained the situation about 10% of COVID-19 sufferers needed intensive care and media sources reported that hospitals and intensive care units in this region had reached its maximum capacity.
Currently, COVID-19 case receipt data in hospitals and/or intensive care units at the EU/EAA level is only available for 6% and 1% of cases (data is not shown).
However, the data must be collected in a systematic way to complete the current surveillance data that focuses on the number of reported cases and death rates.
Studies conducted in 2010-2011 show a huge variation in terms of the availability of intensive care and middle-treatment beddies in Europe, ranging from 29.2 in Germany to 4.2 per 100,000 populations in Portugal.
This means that countries can have more or fewer resources compared to Italy (12.5 intensive care and mid-care beds per 100,000 populations in 2010-2011).
Scenario-related modeling related to saturation of health care capacity, with estimation of inpatient COVID-19 prevaluation cases for any EU/EEA and U.S. countries related to risks >90% beyond intensive care bed capacity, is provided in the sixth update of ECDC quick risk valuation on COVID-19.
Because so far cases are clustered in certain areas in EU/EEA countries and the UK, and hospitals and intensive care units usually serve a population coverage of certain regional services, information about cases and intensive care beds should be provided at the Naming Level of Territories for Statration for Statration 2 (NUTS-2).
Experience from Italy and current trends in other countries indicate that the COVID-19 pandemic is expanding very quickly in EU/EEA and the UK.
Therefore, countries, hospitals, and intensive care units should prepare themselves for SARS-CoV-2 community transmission scenarios and increase the number of COVID-19 patients who need health care, in particular intensive care, as happens in affected areas in Italy.
As demonstrated in a recent ECDC rapid risk assessment, a fast, proactive, and comprehensive approach is critical to slowing the spread of SARS-CoV-2, with the transition from a degradation approach to a mitigation approach, due to an increase in the rapid number of cases, as anticipated, it may not give decision-makers and hospitals enough to understand, accept, and adapt a proper response if it was not implemented in advance.
Such rapid risk assessments also list public health measures to mitigate pandemic impacts.
There's a short window of opportunity when the country has the possibility to further increase its control efforts in slowing the spread of SARS-CoV-2 and reduce pressure on health care.
If it fails, another EU/EEA country's health care system is likely to deal with a surge in patients who require intensive care in the next few days or weeks.
Outbreak of the 2019 coronavirus disease (COVID-19), which is caused by the coronavirus severe acute respiratory syndrome 2 (SARS-CoV-2), has so far killed more than 3,000 people and infects more than 80,000 people in China and other places in the world, which causes a catastrophe for humans.
Similar to its homologous virus, SARS-CoV, which causes SARS in thousands of people in 2003, SARS-CoV-2 may also be transmistagraded from bats and causes similar symptoms through similar mechanisms.
However, COVID-19 has a lower level of severity and Mortality than SARS, but is more contagious and more about the elderly than young people, as well as more in men rather than women.
In response to a rapidly increasing number of publications about this newly emerging disease, this article tries to provide a timely and comprehensive review of fast-developing research subjects.
We will discuss the basics of epidemiology, etiology, virology, diagnosis, treatment, prognosis, and disease prevention.
Although there are still many unanswered questions, we hope this review helps with understanding and eradicating these threatening diseases.
The Spring Festival on January 25, 2020 has been an unacsured and unforgettable remembrance remembrance for all the Chinese who are instructed to stay at home during the holidays and a few weeks thereafter due to an outbreak of a new virus.
This virus is very homologous with the Coronavirus (CoV) which caused an outbreak of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) in 2003; because of that, the virus was named SARS-CoV-2 by the World Health Organization (WHO) on February 11, 2020, and the disease it caused is called CoV Disease-19 (COVID-19).
Epidemic began in Wuhan, China, and quickly spread throughout the country and to almost 50 other countries around the world.
Until March 2, 2020, the virus has resulted in 80,000 confirmed COVID-19 cases, with more than 40,000 patients repatriated and 3,000 patients dying.
WHO warns that COVID-19 is the "negotiem of the number 1 audience" and has the potential to damage more strongly than terrorism.
According to PubMed (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/), in less than two months since the first report on January 7, 2020 which established a sequence of viruses isolated from several patients, more than 200 papers have been published about COVID-19, which includes virology, epidemiology, etiology, disacrimination, and treatment.
This review strives to summarize research advances in this fast-developing new subject field.
As long as possible, we will try to compare COVID-19 with SARS and other CoV-induced diseases, namely Middle Eastern respiratory syndrome (MERS, outbreak in 2012).
We'll also discuss the things that have been studied so far regarding the prevention and prognosis of the disease as well as some other unanswered, but pressing questions.
Based on the tradition, CoV is considered a non-lethal pathogen for humans, it mainly causes about 15% cold 4.
However, in this century, we have encountered highly pathogenic CoV for humans twice, namely SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, each causing an outbreak that originated in China in 2003 and Saudi Arabia in 2012, and immediately spread to many other countries with terrible morbidity and mortality.
Because of this, COVID-19 is currently the third CoV outbreak in recorded human history.
As shown in Gbr. 1.1, pneumonia clusters with unacquaintible origin are first reported from Wuhan on December 31, 2019 to the National Health Commission of the People's Republic of China.
Seven days later the CoV sekusen was launched.
As of January 15, in 2020 the first fatal case from Wuhan was reported.
Meanwhile, the epidemic spreads rapidly to cities, provinces, and neighboring countries.
As of January 20, a health care provider's infection was reported, which hinted that human-to-human-to-human transmission was possible.
On January 23, territorial quarantine was enforced in the city of Wuhan and the entire public transport was halted.
As of January 24, the first clinical study on the disease reported that, out of 41 patients with confirmed cases, only 21 patients were in direct contact with the Wuhan seafood market which was considered to be the initial location of infection from unknown animal sources.
On January 30, WHO declared the outbreak a global health emergencies.
At the time this report was written, the disease had already spread throughout China and nearly 50 other countries around the world (Gr. 2).
Because the situation progresses very quickly, the final coverage and the severity of the outbreak must still be established.
On February 11, 2020, a multi-centered study of 8,866 patients, which includes 4,021 confirmed COVID-19 patients, present a picture of the following more updated epidemic (https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/UlBi-HX_rHPXa1qHA2bhdA).
SARS-CoV-2 infects people of all ages, but is mainly at the age of 30-65.
Nearly half (47.7.7%) of infected people are more than 50 years old, only a few are under the age of 20, and only 14 people are infected who are under the age of 10.
SARS-CoV-2 infects men (0.31/100,000) than women (0.27/100,000).
COVID-19 is widespread in clusters, especially in and around Hubei.
COVID-19 takes an average of 5 (2--9) days ranging from on-devices to diagnosis.
An average incubation period is 4.8 (3.0-7,2) days.
The average time ranging from on-to-device is 9.5 (4,8–13).
The basic reproduction rate (R0) is 3.77 (95% CI: 3,51-4.05) and the squared R0 of 2.23-4.82.
The number of infected people increased exponentially before Jan. 23. 2020, in accordance with massive transport times prior to the Spring Festival in China.
Mortality of patients with confirmed cases of 1.44% (95% CI: 1.10-1.86%) and multiplied mortality for all patients by 3.06% (95% CI: 2.02-4.59%).
The three main risk factors for COVID-19 are sex (male), age (≥60 years), and severe pneumonia.
CoV is a subfamily subfamili of a large-covered virus that contains a single camel RNA sense.
CoV can be divided into four genera, namely alpha, beta, gamma, and delta, with alfa- and beta-CoV known to infect humans.
Glicoprotein bulge or spike (S) cover binds to its cell receptor, angiotensin-exposuiring enzyme 2 (ACE 2) and peptivil peppidase 4 (DPP4) each for SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, then membrane fusion occurs.
Viral RNA genomics are released into the cytoplasm; after retaliation of the viral genome, genomic RNA accompanied cover glycoprotein and nucleocappsid proteins form virion-filled vesicles, which then fusion with the plasma membrane to release the virus.
First SARS-CoV-2 genomic sequence was reported on January 10, 2020.
SARS-CoV-2 is a new beta-CoV type with more than 99.98% genetic similarities among the 10 suspended samples; the sample was taken from the location of the outbreak, the Huanan Seafood Market in Wuhan.
Genetically, SARS-CoV-2 is more similar to SARS-CoV compared to MERS-CoV.
Via transmission electron micromiology, SARS-CoV-2 particles are found in the epithelium part of a very thin human airway.
Human ACE2 was found to be a receptor for SARS-CoV-2 as well as SARS-CoV.
However, the SARS-CoV-2 protein binds to human ACE2 is weaker than its bonds with the SARS-CoV protein, according to the fact that SARS-CoV-2 causes infection that is not as severe as SARS-CoV infection in patients.
SARS-CoV-2 can also form new short proteins that are encoded with orf3b and proteins that are celestituted by orf8 ciphers.
Orf3b SARS-CoV-2 proteins may play a role in virus pathogenicity and IFNβ expression inhibition; however, orf8 does not have known functional domains or motifs.
As of February 18, 2020, Zhou, et al. reported a complete human ACE2 cryo-EM structure at a resolution of 2.9 Å in a complex with B0AT1 an amino acid transporter.
They found that the complex, which had an open and enclosed conform, was arranged to form dimer and the ACE2-B0AT1 complex could bind to two S proteins, which became evidence for CoV recognition and infection.
B0AT1 can be used as a therapeutic target in flapping drugs to suppress SARS-CoV-2 infection.
Host origins and intermediaries
SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV are known to be derived from bats and each is transmitted to humans through ferns and camels.
Going through a philogenetic comparison of SARS-CoV-2 with another CoV, the bat is thought to be the original host SARS-CoV-2 because this new virus is 96% synonymous with the two SARS-like CoVS of the bats called bat-SL-CoVX45 and bat-SL-CoVZX21.
However, intermediary hosts that help the virus cross the species barrier to invade humans is still unknown, and the transmission route must still be explained.
Ji, et al. proposing the serpent as the carrier of the virus from the bat to humans involving homologous recombination in the S protein.
According to a study, researchers in Guangzhou, China, proposed that pangolins, an ant-eating-eating long-measing mammals are often used in traditional Chinese remedial medicines, potentially a SARS-CoV-2 intermediary host based on 99% genetic homologies in the CoV found in pangolins and SARS-CoV-2.
However, the 1% difference spread throughout the genome is still a big difference; therefore, concluclusive results as concrete evidence remain unaccommodated (Fiqed. 3).
SARS-CoV-2 physicochemical properties are largely unknown.
In vitro, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV can last for 48 hours in dry environments and up to 5 days at 20°C and 40%-50% humidity.
SARS-CoV-2 may have similar properties.
It is reported that SARS-CoV-2 is sensitive to Ultraviolet ray and heat at 56°C for 30 minutes; ether, ethanol 75%, disinfectant containing chlorine, perseetic acid, cloroform, and other fat solvents, but not chlorhexidine, effectively inactivating the virus.
In general, the entire human population does not have immunity against SARS-CoV-2 and is therefore susceptible to this new virus.
Currently, there has not been a detailed study reporting on an immunological response to SARS-CoV-2.
Therefore, we can only refer to previous CoV research, in particular SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV (Gr. 4).4).
In general, after invading the host, the virus is recognized by the congenital immune system through pattern-recognizing (PRR), which includes C-type lectin-like receptor, Toll-like receptor (TLR), NOD-like receptor (NLR), and RIG-I-like receptor (RLR).
Through different pathways, the virus induces flammatorial effects, dendritic maturation, and interferon synthesis type I (IFN) that limits the spread of the virus and accelerating viral antigen phagocytosis by macrophages.
However, N protein in SARS-CoV can help the virus evacuate an immune response.
It wasn't long before, an adaptive immune response merged into a battle against this virus.
Lymphocytes T, which include CD4+ and CD8+ cells, play an important role in this defense.
CD4+ T-cells stimulate B cells to produce antibodies that are specific to the virus, and CD8+ T cells kill virus-infected cells directly.
Auxiliary T cells produce cytokine proinflamatory to help protect cells.
However, CoV can inhibit the function of T cells by inducing the apoptosis of T cells.
Humorial immunity, which includes complements, such as C3a and C5a, as well as antibodies are also important in fighting viral infections.
For example, the Isolated antidollator of a cured-healed patient neutralizes MERS-CoV.
On the other hand, an excessive immune system reaction produces a large number of free radicals on a local basis, which can cause severe damage to the pulmonaries and other organs, and, in worst-case scenarios, compound organ failure and even death.
SARS-CoV-2 infection, which is characterized by an onslaught plagiarism, affects the elderly with incorporation diseases and pregnant women.
In general, people who are exposed to large amounts of the virus or whose immunicated immune function has a higher chance of infection than anyone else.
Estimation of SARS-CoV-2's average incubation period is 1-14 days, mostly 3-7 days based on the study of the first 425 cases in Wuhan.
However, a study of 1,099 cases shows that the average incubation period is 3 days and ranges from 0 to 24 days.
A newer study, as described above, showed that the incubation period of 4.8 (3,0-7.2) days based on the demographic of 8,866 cases.
It's very important for health authorities to adjust effective quarantine time based on the most accurate incubation period so as to prevent an infected, but asymptomized person, transmitting the virus to others.
As a general practice, people who are exposed to the virus or infected are usually required to be quarantined for 14 days.
Should quarantine time be extended for up to 24 days?
Fever is often a symptom of major COVID-19 and in the beginning, with or uncompanied by other symptoms such as dry cough, shortness of breath, muscular pain, congestion, headache, sore throat, rinore, chest pain, diarrhoea, diarrhoea, nausea, and vomiting.
Some patients experience dyspnea and/or hypoxemia one week after the onslaught of the disease.
In strenuous cases, the development of the patient's disease takes place quickly into acute respiratory syndrome, septic shock, metabolic acidosis, and coagulopathy.
Patients who experience fever and/or symptoms of breathing and acute fever, although without pulmonary immersive abnormalities, should be filtered against the virus for an early diagnosis.
Demographic studies in late December of 2019 showed that the percentage of symptoms amounted to 98% for fever, 76% for dry cough, 55% for dyspnea, and 3% for diarrhoea; 8% of patients required ventilator support.
Similar findings were reported in two recent studies, namely studies in family clusters and clusters caused transmission from asymptomatic people.
Comparable to that, demographic studies in 2012 showed that MERS-CoV patients also experienced fever (98%), dry cough (47%), and dyspnea (55%) as their main symptoms.
However, 80% of patients need ventilation support, much more than COVID-19 patients and are consistent with higher MERS lethality than COVID-19.
Diarrhea (26%) and sore throat (21%) are also observed in MERS patients.
In SARS patients fever (99%-100%), dry cough (29%-75%), dispnea (40%-42%), diarrhoea (20-25%), and sore throats (13-25%) are shown as major symptoms and ventilation support are needed for about 14%-20% of patients.
Until February 14, COVID-19 Mortality of 2% with confirmed cases reached 66,576 worldwide.
Comparable to that, SARS mortality in November 2002 amounted to 10% from 8,096 confirmed cases.
For MERS, based on a demographic study in June 2012, mortality amounted to 37% from 2,494 confirmed cases.
The previous study reported that R0 SARS-CoV-2 reached as high as 6.47 with 95% confidence interval (CI) of 5.71-7.23, while R0 SARS-CoV only ranged from 2 to 4.
Comparison of SARS-CoV-2 with MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV in terms of symptoms, Mortality, and R0 given in Table 1.
These figures show that SARS-CoV-2 has higher disseminating capabilities than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV, but it does not look at MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV.
Because of that, the challenge to control the SARS-CoV-2 pandemic is greater than the MERS-CoV pandemic and SARS-CoV.
Clusters are often found in one family or from a gathering or vehicle such as a yacht.
Patients often have a history of travel or living in Wuhan or other affected areas or in contact with an infected person or patient in the last two weeks before the onslaught.
However, it is reported that people can carry the virus without showing symptoms for more than two weeks and patients being discharged from the hospital can carry the virus again. This causes vigilance to increase quarantine time.
In the initial stage, the patient has a normal (let alone lymphocythic) of normal or decreased lymphocytes.
For example, limfopenia with white blood cell count < 4×109/L, which includes  lymphocyte counting < 1×109/L, aspartate aminotransferase and increased viremia levels found in 1.099 COVID-19 patients.
Levels of liver and muscle enzymes as well as myoglobin increase in the blood of a number of patients, as well as C-reactive proteins and blood precipitating rates increase in most patients.
In patients with severe cases, D-dimer levels are increased, namely fibrin decomposation products present in the blood, and calculating limphocytes decreased progressively.
Torax photo abnormalities are found in most COVID-19 patients and are characterized by bilateral cloudy shadows or ground-glass opasities in the lungs.
Patients often experience atypical pneumonia, acute pulmonary pulmonary injury, and Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).
When ARDS occurs, untagonized inflammation, fluid accumulation, and progressive fibrosis greatly aggravate the disruption of gas exchange.
Pneumocyte Dysfunction type I and type II lowers surfactant levels and increases surface pressure so as to decrease the ability of the lungs to inflate and increase the risk of pulmonary collapses.
Because of this, the worst-farm findings in thoracic photos are often parallel to the most severe levels of the disease.
On February 18, 2020, the pathological analysis of COVID-19 first demonstrated pneumocyte desquamation, hyaline membrane formation, and interstitial limphocyte infiltration, as well as many-core synsitial cells in the pulmonaries of patients who died due to this disease, in accordance with the pathology of viral and ARDS infection as well as similar to the pathological analysis of SARS and MERS patients.
SARS-CoV-2 RNA detection through a transcipitate transcription (RT (RT-PCR) is used as a key criterion for the diagnosis of COVID-19.
However, due to the high levels of false negligence, which can accelerate the epidemic, clinical manifestations began to be used for the diagnosis (which no longer depends only on RT-PCR) in China on February 13, 2020.
A similar situation also occurs in the diagnosis of SARS.
Because of this, the combination between disease history, clinical manifestations, laboratory tests, and radiological findings is very important and a necessity to create an effective diagnosis.
As of February 14, 2020, Feng Zhang's group described the CRISPR-based SHERLOCK technical use protocol to detect SARS-CoV-2, which detects synthetic SARS-CoV-2 RNA fragments at 20×10-18 moles/L to 200×10-18 mol/L (10--100 copies per microliter input) using dipping sticks in less than an hour without requiring complicated instrumentation.
Hopefully, this new technique can greatly increase the sensitivity and ease if it verifitates in clinical samples.
Due to the lack of experience with new CoVs, doctors primarily offer supporting treatments to COVID-19 patients, while trying various therapies that have been used or previously proposed for other CoV treatments, such as SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV, and other viral diseases (Table 2).
This therapy includes current therapies and potential therapies with antiviral drugs, immunosuppressant, steroids, plasma from cured patients, traditional Chinese medicine, and psychological support.
Even the plasma of a cured patient is proposed to be used in therapy.
Pharmaceutical companies are racing to develop antibodies and vaccinated against this virus.
In the early stages, SARS-CoV-2 mainly attacks the lungs and may also attack other organs that express ACE2 in lower levels, such as the gastrointestinal and kidney systems.
Respiratory malfunctions and malvulneracies are a major threat to the patient and the leading cause of death.
Therefore, respiratory support is very important for relieving symptoms and life-saving, which includes general oxygen therapy, high-circuit flow, non-invasive ventilation, and invasive mechanical ventilation, depending on the severity of the disease.
Patients with severe respiratory symptoms should be supported by the oxygenation of the extractorporeum membrane (ECMO), i.e. motivated cardipulmonary shortness technique used for the treatment of life-threatening heart or respiratory failure.
In addition, the maintenance of electrolyte balance, the prevention and treatment of septic infections, as well as the protection of vital organ functions are also very important for SARS-CoV-2 patients.
Excessive immune system reactions are known to result in cytokine storms in SARS and MERS patients.
Cytokine storm is a form of systemic flammatory response characterized by the release of a series of cytokines, which include TNFα, IL-1β, IL-2, IL-6, IFNα, IFNβ, IFNγ, and MCP-1.
These cytokine-cytokines induce immune cells to release a large number of free radicals which are the main causes of ARDS and failed compound organs.
Immunosupression is very important in the treatment of cytokine storms, especially in severe patients.
Corticosteroids and Tocilizumabubs, a monoclonal anti-IL6 antibodies, have been used to treat cytokine storms.
Other immusophuratory treatments for cytokine storms include modulation of immune-directed immune-response T cells; IFN-γ, IL-1, and TNF impurities; Blinatumomab inhibitors; cytokine signal suppressants 4; and HDAC inhibitors.
Steroid, as an immunosuppressant, is widely used in SARS treatment to reduce the severity of inflammatorial damage.
However, high-dose steroids are not useful for severe lung injury in SARS and COVID-19 patients.
Conversely, steroids can result in severe side effects, especially osteonecrosis, which greatly affects the prognosis.
However, giving short corticosteroids at low to moderate doses is recommended to be administered with caution in critically ill COVID-19 patients.
At the time of writing this review, there has been no effective antiviral therapy that has been confirmed.
However, intravenous remdesivir, a nucleotide analogue, is found efficacy in an American COVID-19 patient.
Remdesivir is a new antiviral drug developed by Gilead, originally for the treatment of diseases caused by the Ebola and Marlburg viruses.
Lately, remdesivir has also shown inhibition of other single-stranded RNA viruses, which include MERS and SARS viruses.
Based on this, Gilead provides this compound for China to carry out a series of trials on people infected SARS-CoV-2, and the results are highly anticipated.
In addition, baricitinb, interferon-α, lopinavir/ritonavir, and ribavirin have been advised as potential therapies for patients with acute respiratory symptoms.
Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, liver damage, and other side reactions can occur after combined therapy with lopinavir/ritonavir.
The interaction of this therapy with other drugs used by the patient should be carefully monitored.
Plasma from patients who have cured and the manufacture of antibodies
There's been a long history of blood collection of patients who have been cured of infectious diseases to treat other patients suffering from the same disease or to prevent healthy people from being exposed to the disease.
Indeed, patients who have been cured often have relatively high antibody levels in the blood.
Antibodies are immunoglobulins (Ig) produced B limphocytes to fight pathogens as well as other foreign objects, recognize unique molecules in pathogens, and directly neutralize them.
Based on this, the plasma was collected from the blood of a group of patients who had recovered from COVID-19 and was injected into 10 severely ill patients.
Their symptoms improved within 24 hours, accompanied by decreased inflammatoryness and residency of the virus as well as an increased saturation of oxygen in the blood.
However, verification and clarification it is necessary to propose such methods for large-scale use before specific therapies are developed.
Furthermore, in addition to its therapeutic effects, some deficiencies related to plasma should be carefully considered.
For example, antibodies can overstimulate the immune-respondence and cause cytokine release syndrome, which is a potentially life-threatening toxicity.
Concentration of antibodies in the blood is usually low and plasma is needed in large quantities to treat critically ill patients.
The development and production of specific antibodys quickly enough to fight a global epidemic is difficult.
Because of this, it is more important and practical to insolate B cells from patients who have healed and identify the genetic code that encodes effective antibodies or perform effective anti-finishing antibodies against proteins that are important for the virus.
In this way, we can easily increase antibody-producing scale.
TCM has been used to treat various diseases in China for thousands of years.
However, the effect largely depends on the combination of different components in the formula. This formula varies depending on the diagnosis of the disease based on TCM’s theory.
Most effective components are still unacquainted or unexplowed because it is difficult to extract or verify such components or their optimal combination.
Currently, because effective and specific therapies for COVID-19 don't yet exist, TCM is becoming one of the main alternative therapies for patients with mild to moderate symptoms or for people recovering from severe illness.
For example, Shu Feng Jie Du's capsule and Lian Hua Qing Wen's capsule were found to be efficacious for COVID-19 therapy.
The top healing levels in COVID-19 patient therapy are observed in some Chinese provinces that use TCM in 87% of its patients, which include Gansu (63.7%), Ningxia (50%), and Hunan (50%), while Hubei province, which uses TCM at only about 30% of COVID-19 patients, has the lowest cure rate (13%).
However, this comparison is still very rude because there are many other impact factors, such as the amount and severity of the patient, should be included in the evaluation.
On February 18, 2020, Boli Zhang and his comrades impaired a study comparing western drug therapy (WM) alone with the combined therapy of WM and TCM.
They found that the time needed for body temperature recovery, loss of symptoms, and hospitalization is very much shorter in the WM+TCM group compared to WM groups alone.
Even more impressive, the rate of symptom-depreciation (than-sleeved to severe) is much lower for the WM+TCM group only (7.4% versus 46.2%) and lower mortality in the WM+TCM group compared to WM alone (8.8% versus 39%).
However, TCM efficacy and safety are still waiting for better controlled trials on a larger scale and in more research centers.
Another interesting thing is to characterize the working mechanism and clarify effective components in TCM therapy or its combination, if possible.
The vast majority of patients suspected or confirmed COVID-19 felt very frightened with this highly contagious and even deadly disease, and quarantined people also felt boredom, loneliness, and anger.
Furthermore, symptoms of infections such as fever, hypoxia, and cough as well as side effects of therapy such as corticosteroid-induced insomnia can lead to greater anxiety and mental stress.
In the early stages of SARS outbreak, a series of psychiatric morbidities, which include depression, anxiety, franic attacks, psychomotor turmoil, psychotic, delirium, and even susidacilities have been reported.
Proliminating contact and quarantine narration, as part of a public health response to the COVID-19 outbreak, can make people become more anxious and feel guilty regarding the effects of contagion, quarantine, and the stigma about relatives and friends.
Therefore, mental health care should be provided for COVID-19 patients, suspected people, and people who are in contact with them, as well as the general public in need.
Psychological support should include the formation of a multidisciplinary mental health team, clear communication with routine and accurate updates about the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak, and a therapy plan as well as the use of professional electronic devices and applications to avoid close contact with each other.
Efficient vaccination is very important to break the chain of transmission from animal reservoirs and humans are infected to vulnerable hosts and are often complementing antiviral therapies in controlling the epidemic caused by the emergence of the virus.
Efforts have been made to develop S-protein-based vaccines, long-term and effectively making neutralizing antibodies and/or immunity that protect against SARS-CoV.
For SARS, life-weakened vaccines have been evaluated on experimental animals.
However, the efficacy in vivo of these vaccine candidates in the elderly and the lettral challenge model as well as its protection against zoonotic viral infections must still be determined before the clinical study begins.
This is probably because SARS subdued 17 years ago and there has not been a new case reported since then.
Conversely, sporadic cases and MERS clusters continue to occur in the Middle East and are spreading to other regions due to the continued existence of zoonotic sources in endemic areas.
Vaccination strategies have been developed for MERS using deactivated viruses, DNA plasmids, virus vectors, nanoparticles, virus-like particles, and recombinant protein subunits and have been partially evaluated on experimental animals.
Vaccine development against SARS-CoV-2 that is safe and effective for non-immune people is a very urgent and important task to control an ongoing epidemic.
However, these prediggerations aren't easily overcome due to a long time to develop the vaccine (averaging 18 months) and dynamic CoV variations.
As a new disease, COVID-19 is just beginning to show its full clinical journey to thousands of patients.
In most cases, the patient can gradually resolve without a sequel.
However, similar to SARS and MERS, COVID-19 is also associated with high morbidity and mortality in patients with severe cases.
Therefore, the formation of disease prognosis models is very important for health care agencies to prioritize their services, especially in areas with limited resources.
Based on clinical studies reported so far, the following factors may influeve or be associated with the culpology of COVID-19 patients (Table 3):
Age: Age is the most important factor for SARS prognosis, which also applies to COVID-19.
COVID-19 mainly occurs in the age of 30-65 years with 47.7% of patients over the age of 50 in the study containing 8,866 cases as described above.
Patients who need intensive care tend to have comorbidituation and compartmental compartmentality as well as significantly older than patients who do not need intensive care (mediments aged 66 years versus 51 years of age), which indicates age as a prognostic factor for the final results of COVID-19 patients.
Gender: SARS-CoV-2 infects more men than women (0.31/100,000 than 0.27/100,000), as described above.
Comorbidities and complications: COVID-19 Patients who require intensive care have a greater chance of experiencing acute heart injury and arrhythmia.
Cardimonial incidence is also the main reason for death in SARS patients.
SARS-CoV-2 is reported can also bond with colangiocytes with ACE2-positive, which can result in liver dysfunction in COVID-19 patients.
It should be noted that age and the underlying disease are very correlated and can affect each other.
Abnormal laboratory findings: C-reactive (CRP) protein levels in the blood reflect inflammatory severity or tissue injury and are proposed as potential prognostic factors for the disease, response to therapy, and final recovery.
Correlation between CRP levels and severity as well as COVID-19 prognosis has also been proposed.
In addition, an increase in lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), aspartate aminotransferase (AST), alanin aminotransferase (ALT), and creatine kinase (CK) can also help predict final results.
These enzymes are highly expressed in a variety of organs, especially in the heart and liver, and are released during tissue damage.
Because of this, these enzymes are traditional markers for heart and liver dysfunction.
Main clinical symptoms: thoracic photos and the development of temporal clinical symptoms should be considered in conjunction with other things to predict the end result and complications of COVID-19.
The use of steroids: As explained above, steroids are immunosuppressants that are widely used as supporting therapies for infectious diseases to reduce the severity of inflamatory damage.
Because high-dose corticosteroids are widely used in severe SARS patients, a lot of people who survived this disease end up suffering an avascular osteonecrosis with a lifelong disability and poor quality of life.
Therefore, if needed, the steroid should be used at low doses and a short period of time in COVID-19 patients.
Mental stress: As explained above, during the COVID-19 outbreak, many patients suffer tremendous stress because they have to undergo long-term quarantine and extreme uncertainty as well as witness the deaths of close family members and fellow patients.
Long-term psychological counseling and long-term support should be provided to help these patients recover from stress and get back to a normal life.
Based on demographic studies so so far, COVID-19 seems to have an epidemiological picture that is different to SARS.
In addition to reclining in the lower respiratory tract, SARS-CoV-2 can replicate efficiently in the upper respiratory tract and cause minor or asymptomatic symptoms in the early phases of the infection, similar to other CoVs that cause colds.
Therefore, in the initial phase or incubation period, infected patients can produce large amounts of the virus during daily activities, which cause great difficulty to control the epidemic.
However, SARS-CoV transmission is believed to occur when the patient is terminally ill and mostly does not occur in the initial phase.
Thus, the current COVID-19 outbreak is much more severe and difficult to control than the SARS outbreak.
At the present time, great efforts are being made in China, which includes quarantine regions of Wuhan and surrounding cities as well as sustainable quarantine of almost the entire population in hopes of severing SARS-CoV-2 transmission.
Although this action is very damaging to the economy and other sectors in the country, the number of new patients is declining, which indicates an epidemic slowdown.
Estimation is most optimistic stating the outbreak will end in March and the reduction phase lasts for 3-4 months.
However, some other experts aren't as optimistic.
Paul Hunter et al. estimated that COVID-19, which appears to be much more contagious than SARS, won't end in 2020.
Ira Longini, et al. makes a model to predict the end result of the epidemic and states that SARS-CoV-2 can infect two-thirds of the global population.
The Canadian research group reported that SARS-CoV-2 was detected in mid-konka swipe and throat of patients who had healed and returned from the hospital 2 weeks in advance. This indicates that re-identifiated viruses can become recurrent episodes, similar to influenza.
However, promising signs occur in China, when viewed based on the declining number of new cases, which indicate that the current strategy may be successful.
Ebola was originally predicted to lead to a million cases with half death.
However, with quarantine and strict insitution, the disease can finally be controlled.
It's also possible, similar to SARS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2 weakens its ability to infect and eventually disappears or becomes a not-so-so-pathic virus that can co-exide with humans.
Comparison of COVID-19 epidemics with SARS and MERS epidemics is given below (Gbr. 5).
SARS-CoV-2 is very easy to transmit through coughing or sneezing, and it may also be through direct contact with materials contaminated by the virus.
This virus is also found in feces so as to give rise to the possibility of a new transmission, namely fecycle-to-mouth transmission.
Recent studies in 138 cases reported that 41% of cases may be caused by a nosokomial infection, which includes 17 patients with other previous diseases and 40 health care providers.
Because of this, careful preventive measures must be taken to protect humans, in particular health care providers, social workers, family members, co-workers, and even people who happen to contact with an infectious patient or person.
The first defense line that can be used to lower the risk of infection is through the use of face masks, both surgical masks and N95 respirator masks (the 1860’s series number) help control the spread of the virus.
Surgical maswers keep sprinkling sprinkling of people who are potentially infected do not contain air or attached to the surface of the material, the sprink sprinkling can be passed on to others.
However, only N95 masks (the 1860's serial number) can protect against virion inhalation with as small sizes as 10 to 80 nm, with only 5% virions can neutralize fully; SARS-CoV-2 is similar to SARS-CoV in terms of size, both measuring about 85 nm.
Because the particles can enetrarate even five surgical masks that are piled into one, health care providers who are in direct contact with the patient must wear N95 (1860's series number) instead of surgical masks.
In addition to masks, health care providers should wear the right solitary cloak to further reduce contact with the virus.
The virus can also infect a person through the eyes.
On January 22, 2020, a doctor was infected with SARS-CoV-2 although he was wearing an N95 mask; the virus might get into his body through eye inflammatory.
Therefore, health care providers should also wear translucent face shields or glasses while working with patients.
For the general public in affected or potentially affected areas, it is highly recommended for everyone to wash their hands with disinfected soap more often than usual, try to stay at home for self-quarantine quarantine, and avoiding contact with potentially infected people.
The distance of three feet is believed to be suitable for maintaining distance from the patient.
These actions are an effective method to reduce the risk of infection as well as to prevent the spread of the virus.
Although SARS-CoV-2 is a new virus for the human world, its high homology with SARS-CoV, as reported on January 7, 2020, should have been a major commemoration for China having strong remembrance of the 2003 SARS outbreak.
However, as of January 19, 2020 the director of the Wuhan Center for Disease Control calmed the population by saying that this new virus has low-spreading properties and limited reproductive capacity from humans to humans and the prevention and deflection of this disease is not a problem.
This message is soothing the fear of the public, especially when the rest of the country is preparing for the Spring Festival, so this critical time is missed to stem a small-sized disease in Wuhan.
The disease control agency in China should confisctuate a lesson from this and make great improvements in the future.
For example, this body should (1) be more careful when making public announcements because every word is noticed by the population and can change their attitudes and decisions; (2) be more sensitive and reactive to unorthodox information from the clinic rather than waiting for an official report from a doctor or authorized officer; (3) it is more restrictive to stem the potential of the epidemic at an early stage, and not to try to calm the public; and (4) more often issue targeted and effective exercises to increase public awareness about disease epidemics and test and improve community response systems on a periodic basis.
COVID-19 outbreaks are caused by a new SARS-CoV-2 virus that began to spread at the end of December 2019.
In less than 2 months, the outbreak had spread throughout China and more than 50 countries in the world at the time of writing this review.
Because the virus is very similar to SARS-CoV and COVID-19 and SARS symptoms are also similar, the COVID-19 outbreak gives an impression like the SARS outbreak reoccurs.
However, there are some clear differences between COVID-19 and SARS, which are critical to stem the epidemic and deal with patients.
"COVID-19 is more about the elderly than young people and more about men rather than women, and the death rate is also higher in the elderly than young people.
SARS Mortality is higher than COVID-19 mortality (10.91% versus 1.44%).
COVID-19 Patients transmit the virus even when they are asymptomatic, while SARS patients usually transmit the virus when they are terminally ill. This creates greater difficulty in stemming the spread of COVID-19 compared to SARS.
This explains in part the reasons SARS-CoV-2 is spreading more quickly and wider than SARS-CoV.
Aasi RNA routine to check for SARS-CoV-2 can give negative results in some COVID-19 patients.
On the other hand, patients who have been cured can provide positive results again against the examination of the virus.
These findings greatly increase the risk of spreading the virus.
With the rapid progress of COVID-19 research, some important problems still have to be resolved, namely:
Where did SARS-CoV-2 come from?
Although it was found 96% genetic homology between SARS-CoV-2 and Twice-like CoV-SARS bat, we are still unable to conclude that SARS-CoV-2 is derived from bats.
What animal becomes an intermediary species to transmit the virus from the host of origin, let's say bats, to humans?
Without knowing the answer number 1 and 2, we cannot break the transmission effectively and the outbreak can re-spread at any time.
Although molecular and biochemistry assai models show that SARS-CoV-2 binds to ACE2, how exactly the virus goes into airway cells and results in further pathological changes?
Does this virus also bind to cells that express ACE2 in other organs?
Without a clear answer to these questions, we can't achieve a quick and accurate diagnosis as well as an effective treatment.
How long will this epidemic take?
How does the virus develop genetically during the transmission between humans?
Is this pandemic going to be a pandemean all over the world, ending like SARS, or reoccupied periodically like the flu?
The answer to the above questions and many other statements is very important, but it may take time to find them.
However, no matter how expensive it is, we have no other choice but to stop this epidemic as soon as possible in order to return to a normal life.
Zonotic origin of human coronavirus
Over the course of thousands of years, mutations and adaptations have mobilized co-evolution of the Coronavirus (CoV (CoV), including humans.
Prior to 2003, two human CoV (HCoVs) were known to cause minor illnesses, such as flu.
An outbreak of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) and Middle East respiratory syndrome (MERS) turn things around and reveal how HCoV infection can be very damaging and life-threatening.
Emergence of SARS-CoV-2 in central China at the end of 2019 has made the CoV re-invigorate and surprised us with high transmission, but with lower pathogenicity than its sibling, SARS-CoV.
HCoV infection is zoonosis. Therefore, the understanding of the origin of zoonotic HCoV will be beneficial for us.
Most HCoV is derived from bats. In these animals, the virus is not pathogenic.
Intermediary reservoir hosts some HCoV have also been known.
Identification of animal hosts has direct implications in the prevention of human diseases.
Inquiry of CoV-in-oquill interaction in animals can also provide an important insight into the pathogenesis of CoV in humans.
In this review, we present an overview of current knowledge of seven HCoV, with a focus on the history of its discovery as well as its zoonotic origins and interspecies transmission.
The important thing is, we compare and contact the HCoV from an evolutionary point of view and the recombination of the genome.
CoV (COV-19) disease epidemic is currently being discussed in this context.
In Addition, successful host switching requirements and viral evolutionary implications on the severity of the disease are also highlighted.
Corona virus (CoV) is derived from the Coronaviridae family, which consists of a group of cloak-shemmified RNA viruses, positive, and single-stranded.
This virus has the largest genome with a size of 26 to 32 kilobases between RNA viruses called "CoV", due to its crown-like morphology under an electron microscopy.
Based on the structure, the CoV has undegmented genomes that have similar structuring.
Approximately two-thirds of the genome consists of two large open reading frames (ORF1a and ORF1b) that overlapping. Both are translated into retaliatory polyproteins pp1a and pp1ab.
Polytproteins undergo further processing to form 16 non-structural proteins, called nsp1~16.
The rest of the genome consists of an open-faith disauguration (ORF) for structural proteins, which include a bulge or spike (S), sheath or envelope (E), membrane (M), and nucleoprotein (N).
Numerous complementary proteins that are specific to a certain lineage are also encoded by various CoV lineages.
Based on discrepancies in protein sequences, CoV is classified into four genera (alpha-CoV, beta-CoV, gamma-CoV, and delta-CoV); among the four generades, the genus beta-CoV has the most HCoV and is subdivided into four bloodlines (A, B, C, and D).
Phylogenetic evidence shows that bats and rodents are the source of mostly alpha-coV and beta-CoV genes, while poultry is the main reservoir of gamma-CoV and delta-CoV.
Over the course of thousands of years, the CoV has continuously trampled across the species barrier and several CoVs have become important pathogens in humans.
To this day, there have been seven known human CoV (HCoV).
Among the seven viruses, HCoV-229E and HCoV-NL63 are alpha-CoV.
The other five beta-CoV include HCOV-OC43, HCOV-HKU1, the coronavirus of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV), the Coronavirus Middle East respiratory syndrome (MERS-CoV), and SARS-CoV-2.
HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1, and HCoV-NL63 usually cause minor symptoms, such as colds and/or diarrhosics.
Conversely, SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV, and SARS-CoV-2 are very pathogenic, causing severe lower airway infections in relatively many more patients and have a higher chance of developing into an acute pernassary (ARDS) syndrome (ARDS) and manifestations outside the pulmonary organs.
Strain HCoV-229E, B814, was isolated from the snot of patients who were in the middle of the 1960's.
Since then, more and more knowledge has been gathered through an in-depth study of HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43, both of which cause self-healing symptoms.
In fact, this concept has been widely accepted that HCoV infection is generally harmless, until the SARS outbreak occurs.
The SARS outbreak that occurred in 2003 was one of the most damaging outbreaks in today's history, which infects more than 8,000 people with a rough mortality rate of about 10%.
Ten years later, an outbreak of Middle Eastern respiratory syndrome (MERS) resulted in an ongoing epidemic in the Arabian Peninsula with sporadic dispersal to various other parts of the world.
HCoV's new 2019 (2019-nCoV), hereinafter called SARS-CoV-2, is a caucasative agent of the 2019 coronavirus (COVID-19 disease), which has claimed the lives of more than 3,120 people and infected more than 91,000 people until March 3, 2020.
Alarm is already ringing and the world must prepare itself for an upcoming SARS-CoV-2 pandemic.
The seven HCoVs all have zoonotic origins of bats, rats, or pets.
There's a lot of evidence supporting the evolution of all HCoV from the bat. In the bat, the CoV adapts well and is nonpathogenic, but shows great genetic diversity.
COVID-19 epidemics have posed tremendous medical, scientific, social, and moral challenges for China and the rest of the world.
HCoV zoonotic procuracy provides a framework to understand the natural history, driving power, and limiting factors of displacement between species.
This study can also guide or facilitate the search for reservist, intermediaries, and SARS-CoV-2 acmplifiers, which have important implications in prevention of future dispensing.
In this review, we provide an overview of zoonotic origins, interspecies transmission, and HCoV pathogenesis.
We are specifically highlighting and addressing a common topic that HCoV parent virus is usually nonpathogenic in its natural reservoir, but becomes pathiogenic after transmitting between species to the new host.
We've also reviewed HCoV's evolutionary trends that increased transmission capabilities are often accompanied by a decrease in pathogenicity.
The end result of the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic is also discussed in this context.
CoV animals have been known since the end of the 1930's.
Prior to the slicing HCoV-229E first strain B814 from snot patients exposed to colds, various CoVs had been insulated from a variety of infected animals, which include turkeys, rats, cows, pigs, cats and dogs.
In the last few decades, seven HCoVs have been identified.
Brief summaries of HCoV discovery in chronological order (Table 1) is an informative clue.
Strain HCoV-229E was first insulated from the airway of patients who had an upper air conditioning infection in 1966, which was further adapted to be overgrotted in WI-38 pulmonary cell lines.
Patients infected with HCoV-229E show symptoms of colds, which include headaches, sneezing, lethargy, and sore throats, accompanied by fever and cough seen in 10~20% of cases.
Subsequently in 1967, HCoV-OC43 was insulated from organ cultures and sequential-sequence pathways in the still-milbing mice.
The clinical picture of HCoV-OC43 infection appears to be similar to an infection caused by HCoV-229E, which is symptomatically undisimilated from other respiratory tract pathogenic infections such as influenza A and rhinovirus viruses.
HCOV-229E and HCoV-OC43 are spread throughout the world and tend to be most transmitted during the winter in temperate regions.
Usually, the incubation time of these two viruses is less than one week and is followed by a period of illness for about 2 weeks.
Based on a human volunteer study, healthy people infected with HCoV-229E had a mild blasphemy.
Only a small number of patients with a weakened immune stimuli indicate a major low-sleeved airway infection.
SARS, also known as "atypical pneumonia", is a pandemic due to a well-documented HCoV in human history and its etiological agent is SARS-CoV, which is the third HCoV discovered.
SARS' first case can be traced back to the end of 2002 in Guangdong Province, China.
The SARS epidemic resulted in 8,096 cases reported with 774 deaths, which are spread across many countries and continents.
Apart from a super contagion, each case is thought to cause approximately two secondary cases, with an incubation period of 4 to 7 days and the peak of the viral load is seen on the 10th day of the disease.
Patients infected with SARS-CoV initially experience myalgia, headaches, fever, lethargy, and chills, followed by dyspnea, cough, and respiratory disorders as advanced symptoms.
Limfopenia, an obsordinary test of liver function, and an increase in creatine kinase is a common laboratory malnutrition in SARS.
Alveolar diffusion deterioration deterioration, epitel cell proliferation, and increased macrophages are also observed in SARS patients.
Subsequently, approximately 20-30% of patients require intensive care and mechanical ventilation.
In addition to the lower respiratory tract, some organs that include gastrointestinal tract, liver, and the kidneys can also be infected in severe cases, usually accompanied by cytokine storms, which can be deadly, especially in patients whose immunity is weakened.
The virus was first insulated from an open pulmonary pulmonary pulmary biopsy a relative of zero patients who traveled to Hong Kong from Guangzhou.
Since then, enormous efforts have been dedicated to HCoV research.
HCOV-NL63 was isolated from a 7-month-old child from the Netherlands at the end of 2004.
This virus is initially very prevalent in young children, the elderly, and patients whose immunity suffers from respiratory diseases.
An overview of koriza, conjunctivitis, fever, and bronchiolitis are commonly found in diseases caused by HCoV-NL63.
Another self-introdependent study explains the same viral isolation of an 8-year-old boy's nasal specimen who suffers from pneumonia in the Netherlands.
Although it is identified in the Netherlands, the virus is actually spread all over the world.
It's estimated that HCoV-NL63 represents about 4.7% of common respiratory diseases and the peak of its occurrence occurs in early summer, spring, and winter.
HCoV-NL63 is associated with obstructive laryngitis, which is also referred to as krup.
In the same year, HCoV-HKU1 was insulated from a 71-year-old man who had been hospitalized for pneumonia and bronchiolitis in Hong Kong.
In addition to pneumonia and community bronchiocylitis, HCoV-HKU1 is reportedly associated with the exacerbation of acute asthma.
Similar to HCoV-NL63, HCoV-229EE, and HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 is found all over the world, causing mild respiratory diseases.
Four HCoV communities have entirely adapted to humans and are generally unlikely to mutate into very pathogenic diseases. However, extraordinary cases do occur for unknown reasons, such as in the case of more virulent HCoV-NL63 subtypes that have recently been reported to cause severe lower airway infections in China.
In general, when it gets the ability to move efficiently and continue to maintain itself in the human being, HCoV virulence and pathogenicity is also reduced.
MERS-CoV was first illustaposed in 2012 from a 60-year-old pulmonary patient with acute pneumonia and kidney failure in Saudi Arabia.
Although most laboratory-confirmed cases come from the Middle East, imported cases with occasional secondary dissemination to close contact have been reported in various European and Tunisian countries.
Other secondary outbreaks occurred in South Korea in 2015 with 186 confirmed cases.
MERS clinical manifestations are similar to SARS clinical manifestations, which are characterized by progressive acute pneumonia.
Unlike SARS, many MERS patients also experience acute kidney failure, which so far only occurs in MERS among HCoV-induced diseases.
More than 30% of patients show gastrointestinal symptoms, such as diarrhoea and vomiting.
Until February 14, 2020, more than 2500 laboratory-conffirmed cases were reported with high case deaths, amounting to 34.
In mid to late December of 2019, clusters of pneumonia patients who are retrospectively associated with SARS-CoV-2 infection were found in Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
The World Health Organization (WHO) stated that the outbreak of underwentime airway infection was ongoing and was caused by SARS-CoV-2 as a Global Health Emergency and renamed the disease COVID-19.
Until March 3, 2020, 90,053 cases have been confirmed all over the world, with gross case mortality rate of 3.4%.
It's worth noting, the death of cases in Hubei, China, amounted to 4.2%, while the death of cases outside Hubei amounted to 1.2%.
SARS-CoV-2 causes severe respiratory infections such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV with images of fever, cough, and shortness of breath.
Diarrhea is also found in a number of patients.
Pneumonia is one of the heaviest symptoms and can develop quickly into an acute respiratory syndrome.
Although SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 are very similar due to the homology of high nucleotide sequences, by 82%, they both group into different branches of the philogenetic tree.
SARS-CoV-2 pathogonism is clearly lower, but is more contagious compared to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
Subjects infected with SARS-CoV-2 asymptomatic have been reported and may play a role in the rapid spread of SARS-CoV-2 all over the world.
Comparing and distinguishing SARS-CoV-2 with six other HCoV shows very interesting similarities and discriminates.
First, the incubation period and course duration of HCoV disease is very similar.
In this case, SARS-CoV-2 follows the general trend of six other HCoV.
Second, the severity of COVID-19 lies between SARS-CoV and four other HCoV communities (such as HCoV-229E, HCOV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1, and HCoV-NL63).
On the one hand, SARS-CoV-2 infection exhibits a more commonly seen picture of Community HCoV infection, which includes images of mild non-specific or even asymployment.
On the other hand, a small percentage of severe COVID-19 cases can be seen like SARS-CoV infection alms with a slightly lower ratio.
Third, SARS-CoV-2 transmissions also show interesting pattern characteristics, having a resemblance to HCoV community and SARS-CoV.
On the one hand, SARS-CoV-2 transmission capabilities are at least as high as community HCoV.
On the other hand, deterioration of SARS-CoV-2 transmitting capabilities after several pathways in humans still needs to be verifiable, according to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV cases.
Lastly, just like other HCoV, SARS-CoV-2 can be detected from fecal samples.
"Whemn't fecal-oral SARS-CoV-2 transmission plays an important role, as in the SARS-CoV transmission case, at least in certain situations, it should still be clarified by subsequent studies."
What is also very important to know is whether SARS-CoV-2 is seasonal such as in HCoV community cases.
However, the SARS-CoV-2 picture, which includes the ability of transmission, pathogenicity, and sustained spread in several pathways in humans, will greatly affect the final fate of the ongoing COVID-19 outbreak.
Four HCoV communities that cause mild symptoms have all been adapted to humans.
Seen from another point of view, it can be said that humans have adapted to these four HCoV.
In other words, both HCoV and humans may have had antiquan-itudinal HCoV pandemic survivors.
HCoV that causes severe disease in humans and humans who experience severe HCoV pain has been wiped out.
In order for this to happen, HCoV must be replicated in humans in adequate quantities to enable the accumulation of adaptive mutations to fight host restriction factors.
Thus, the longer the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak persists and the more people are infected, the more likely it is for this virus to fully adapt to humans.
If the virus adapts well, its transmission in humans will be difficult to stop with quarantine or other infection control measures.
Over the years, four community CoVs circulated in the human population and triggered a flu in people who were immunocompetent.
This virus does not require an animal reservoir.
Conversely, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV that are highly pathogenic have not been adapted to humans and its transmission in humans cannot be sustained.
The two must defend themselves and breed in zoonotic reservists as well as look for opportunities to spread to vulnerable human targets, possibly through one or more intermediary hosts and amplifiers.
SARS-CoV-2 has an image similar to SARS-CoV/MERS-CoV and four HCoV communities.
SARS-CoV-2 is very easy to transmissible like HCoV communities, at least now.
However, SARS-CoV-2 is more pathogenic than HCoV community and is not sepatogenic SARS-CoV or MERS-CoV.
It should still be observed whether SARS-CoV-2 will be fully adaptable to humans and circulate in a human without a reservoir animal host or an intermediary.
Before addressing HCoV animal origins, we need to discuss the definitions and characteristics of Evolutionary hosts, natural, reservoirs, intermediaries, and amplifiers of HCoV.
The animal acts as an Evolutionary Host of HCoV if it has an ancestor who is closely related and shares high homologology at the level of nucleotide sequence.
Ancestral viruses usually adapt well and are non-pathogenic in this host.
Similarly, reservoir hosts carry HCoV sustainably and for a long time.
In these two cases, the host is naturally infected and is a natural host for HCoV or the parent virus.
Conversely, if a new HCoV goes into an intermediary host just before or around the time the virus enters humans, the virus has not adapted to a new host and is often pathogenic.
This intermediary host can serve as a zoonotic source of human infection and serves as an amplifying host by allowing the virus to temporarily replicate, then transmit it to humans to amplify the scale of human infections.
HCOV can have a dead-end infection if it is unable to maintain its transmission in an intermediary host.
Conversely, HCoVs can also adapt to intermediary hosts and even lead to long-term endemicity.
In this case, the intermediary host becomes the host of a natural reservoir.
Epidemiological data reveals retrospectively that SARS zero cases have a history of in contact with fugitive animals.
Seroprevalence research further indicates that the veterinary has a higher prevalence of igG anti-SARS-CoV compared to the general population.
Wrought moon (Paguma warta) and raccoon dogs in the living animal market are the first to be identified as carrying a similar-SARS-CoV virus that is almost synonymous with SARS-CoV.
Inauthentictly, this is supported by the fact that there are no more SARS cases have been reported since all the fermbosets in the market were killed.
However, it is reported that a weasel of a month from the wilds or randges unexpressed to such live animal markets largely provides negative test results for SARS-CoV. This hints that weasel months may only act as intermediary amplifying hosts, but are not SARS-CoV natural resorpoirs.
It should be noted, because 80% of the various animals in the Guangzhou market have anti-SARS-CoV antibodies, it can not be ruled out that maybe many small mammal species also serve as an amplifier of SARS-CoV intermediaries.
All of these hosts seem to be SARS-CoV's final hosts.
The search for natural animal hosts for SARS-CoV further revealed a close-douffled CoV, the so-called CoV of Rhinopous blonde relatives-SARS HKU3 (SARSr-Rh-BatCoV HKU3), which is located on the Chinese horseshoe bat.
These bats provide positive test results for anti-SARS-CoV antibodies and SARSr-Rh-BatCoV HKU3 genomic sequens.
This CoV and other bat CoV share homologic nucleotide sequence 88-92% with SARS-CoV.
This study became the foundation for a new concept that bats are hosts for emerging human pathologies.
Some SARS (SL-CoV) is also identified from the bat, but only one, named WV1, can be isolated as a living virus.
Angiotensin 2 (ACE2) converting enzymes are known to be SARS-CoV receptors.
WV1 that comes from a sample of fire fecal faeces is shown using ACE2 bats, ferocious, and humans as receptors to get into cells.
Interestingly, convalescent SARS patient serum can neutralize WIV1.
So far, WIV1 has represented an ancestor enclosed closest to SARS-CoV in a bat, with a homology of nucleotic sequences of 95%.
Apart from the high homologology between these two viruses, it is generally believed that WV1 is not the direct parent virus SARS-CoV and the bat is not a direct reservoir host SARS-CoV.
Phylogenetic analysis groups MERS-CoV into the same group as CoV-HKU4 bats and CoV-HKU5 bats.
CoV-HKU4 bat and MERS-CoV use the same host receptor, i.e. peptidyl peptipidase 4 (DPP4), as a way of entering the virus.
MERS-CoV RNA polymerase sequens are phylomenetically closer to their equivalents in beta-CoV bats identified from Europe and Africa.
Until now, there has been no living MERS-CoV found in wild bats.
MERS-CoV and his closest relatives CoV-HKU25 bats only share 87% homology of nucleotide sequences.
That’s why, the bat may not be an MERS-CoV direct reservoir host.
On the other hand, studies in the Middle East show that Arabic camels provide seropositive results for specific antibodies that neutralize MERS-CoV, just like Middle Eastern camels in some African countries.
MERS-CoV lives synonymous with the virus found in humans isollated from an Arab camel nasal swab swipe. This further indicates that camels are an actual reservoir host for MERS-CoV.
It is also very important to note that the decay of the massive virus with symptoms that are usually mildly observed on camel experiments infected with MERS-CoV.
It should be noted, infected camels shed the virus not only through the respiratory route, but also through the fecal-oral route, which is also the main route of viral decay from the bat.
However, questions remain unanswered because many cases of confirmed MERS do not have a history of contact with camels prior to death symptoms, possibly as a result of human-in-for--human transmission or an unidentified transmission route involving unrecognizable animal species carrying MERS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 shares 96.2% homology of nucleotides with RATG13 blinary CoV isolated from Rhinolophus Affinis bristles.
As in SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV cases, the sequential difference between SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 is too great to establish a parent relationship.
In other words, the bat may not be the direct reservoir host of SARS-CoV-2, except if an almost identical bat CoV is found later in life.
Possibly, SARS-CoV-2 intermediary animal hosts exist among wildlife species that are sold and killed at the Huanan Seafood Wholesaler Market. Many early cases of COVID-19 are related to this market, which indicates the possibility of animal-to-human transmission.
Several recent studies based on metagennomic sequences have shown that a group of small-impressed mamm mammals, known as pangolins (Manis javanica), it may also carry venomous ancestral beta-CoV with SARS-CoV-2.
This new pangolin CoV genome shares 85-92% homologic nucleotide sequence with SARS-CoV-2.
However, the CoV is similarly related to RaTG13 in similarities by 90% at the nucleotide sequence level.
Both are grouped into two subline subline-descendants of SARS-CoV-2-similated in a phylogenetic tree, one of which shares a more-sophisticating (RBD (RBD) domain similar to SARS-CoV-2, with an amino acid sequence similarity of 97.4%.
Concisely, the SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 RBD are much different, although the homologological levels of the entire genome sequence are higher.
A previous study on a diseased pangolin also reported detection of contig virus from pulmonary samples, which turned out to also be related to SARS-CoV-2.
This study adopts different drafting methods and manual curates to produce partial genome sequens consisting of about 86.3% complete viral genomes.
We can't override the possibility of pangolining as one of SARS-CoV-2 intermediate animal hosts.
However, at the moment there has been no evidence supporting SARS-CoV-2 coming directly from pangolins due to the sequential differences between SARS-CoV-2 and beta-CoV relatives-SARS-CoV-2 pangolins.
In addition, the distance between SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 is even closer than the distance between SARS-CoV-2 and beta-CoV relatives - SARS-CoV-2.
The SARS-CoV-2's evolutionary path on bats, pangols, and other mammals must still be established.
Although sequent homologies are highest in RBD found between SARS-CoV-2 and beta-CoV relatives-SARS-CoV-2 pangolins, SARS-CoV-2 and RTTG13 share the highest homologology at the sequencing levels of the entire genome.
It's too speculated if it's said that the high level of resemblance between RBD beta-CoV family-SARS-CoV-2 pangolins and SARS-CoV-2 is driven by a convergent evolution whose electivity is mediated.
Comparison hypotheses support a recombination between beta-coV relatives-SARS-CoV-2 pangolins and RTG13 in third wild animal species.
As an evociable factor of evolution, recombination occurs a lot among beta-CoV.
The researchers are still not able to decide the origin of SARS-CoV-2 zoonotics.
In addition to highly pathogenic HCoV, zoonotic origin HCoV-229FE, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-NL63, and HCoV-HKU1 have also been studied.
Phylogenetical evidence indicates that HCoV-NL63 and HCoV-229E may come from a bat's CoV, while HCoV-OC43 and HCoV-HKU1 parent viruses have been found in rodents.
It is reported that a bat's CoV named ARCoV.2 (Appalachian Ridge CoV) which is detected in the bat three colors of North America shows close kinship with HCoV-NL63.
On the other hand, HCoV-229E is genetically rescinding with another bat's CoV, under the name Hipposideros/GhanaKwam/19/2008, which was detected in Ghana, with Camelidae allegedly also an intermediary.
To make it clearer, the current knowledge of HCoV animal's known origin is summarized in Figures 1 and Table 2.
Phylogenetic analysis has provided evidence of HCoV interspecies transmission events in history.
In about 1890, when HCoV-OC43 passed through species of farm animals to innovate humans, a pandemic of a respiratory infection.
HCoV-229E's interspetion transmission history isn't very clear.
Alfa-CoV bat resilient with HCoV-229E has been found.
In between the two, there's alfa-CoV alpaka.
Some evidence supports the transmission of the virus directly from the bat to humans.
First, humans, not alpaka, who may come into contact with bats in shared ecological recesses.
On the contrary, humans have close contact with alpaka.
Secondly, alpha-CoV bat reciprocated with HCoV-229E is as varied and nonpathogenic in the bat, while alfa-CoV alpaca causes an outbreak of respiratory disease in infected animals.
Finally, alfa-CoV alpaka is not found in ferral animals.
Therefore, it is likely that alpaka getting an alfa-CoV relatives-HCoV-229E from humans cannot be ruled out.
In fact, the bat is a direct source for human pathogenic viruses, which include rabies virus, Ebola virus, Nipah virus, and Hendra virus.
Therefore, it's not too tanthanous that the bat might directly transmit the HCoV-229E to humans.
The other possibility, while the bat's alfa-CoV serves as an ouster of HCoV-229E genes, alpaka and camels may serve as intermediary hosts that transmit the virus to humans, just like the MERS-CoV case.
MERS-CoV is an excellent example for the transmission between spies from the bat to an Arab camel and from an Arab camel to humans.
The evolutionary origin of MERS-CoV from the bat is known in the initial identification of the virus and is also amplified by subsequent findings.
It's clear that the bats are hunch-rich species of viruses for the exchange of genetic fragments interspecies and interspecies transmission.
Long ages, densely arrayed colonies, close social interactions, and strong flying capabilities, are all supportive conditions for bats to become the ideal 'virus spreaders'.
On the other hand, MERS-CoV has gone into an Arab camel for decades.
This virus has been adapted to a camel that has changed from an intermediary host to a stable and natural reservoir host.
MERS-CoV causes a very mild disease and maintains a relatively low rate of mutation in these animals.
Its sporadic transmission to humans occurs coincidentally and humans remain the final host for MERS-CoV because the transmission cannot be sustainable.
Unlike the role of camels in MERS-CoV transmission, the role of pangolins, if there is, in different SARS-CoV-2 transmission.
Primarily, beta-CoV pangolins are highly pathogenic in pangoing.
Pangolins may be the final host for beta-CoV relatives SARS-CoV-2, similar to ferret in SARS-CoV cases.
In future studies, some possible transmission of SARS-CoV-2 interspecies from animals to humans must be taken into account or ruled out.
First, the bat could have been a reservoir host for SARS-CoV-2's relative virus that is almost identical to SARS-CoV-2.
Humans can share ecological recesses with bats through slaughtering or coal mining.
Second, a pangolin could be one of an intermediary amplifying host for a newly incoming SARS-CoV-2 relative virus.
Humans get the virus through slaughter and the consumption of hunting meat.
It's possible, many mamm mammals, which include pets, are susceptible to SARS-CoV-2.
An antibody survey against pets and wild animals needs to be done.
Third, as mentioned above, SARS-CoV-2 recombinations and adaptations may take place in a third-inclusive-contact species with bats and pangolins.
The search of SARS-CoV-2 animal origin is still ongoing.
Apart from the different types of animal hosts, the three main factors in terms of the virus is also important to make it easier for CoV to traverse species barriers.
First, the rate of mutation is relatively high in RNA replication.
Compared to other single-stranded RNA viruses, an approximation of CoV mutation rate can be considered “moderate” to “heavy” with an average substitution rate of ~10-4 per year per treastrophet, depending on the CoV adaptation phase to a new host.
CoV has an exoribonuclease of correctors, deletion resulting in very high mutability, attenuation, or even inviability.
Interestingly, Remdesivir nucleotide an analogue is known to suppress CoV replication through this exoribunuclease inhibition and RNA polymerase-irac.
Remdesivir is one of the most promisproving anti-SARS-CoV-2 agents to be tested in clinical trials.
However, the rate of CoV mutation is about a million times higher than the rate of its host mutation.
In addition, the rate of mutation is often high when the CoV has not adapted to the host.
Compirsed SARS-CoV which has a high mutation rate, SARS-CoV-2 mutation rates are actually lower, implying a higher level of adaptation with humans.
Possibly, SARS-CoV-2 has adapted to other relative hosts close to humans.
In addition to SARS-CoV-2, this also applies to MERS-CoV which has adapted to the Arabic camel.
Theoretically, it's less likely that genetic drift will quickly make vaccines and antiviral agents against SARS-CoV-2 unemployed.
Second, the large RNA genome in the CoV provides an extra flexibility in genome modification, for mutation and recombination, thereby increasing the probability of co-evolution interspecies, which is advantageous for the emergence of a new CoV when conditions are appropriate.
This is supported by the abundance of unique open reading frames and the function of proteins that are encoded towards the 3′ end of the genome.
Third, the CoV often changes random templat during RNA replication through a unique “choself-saline” mechanism.
In the host that serves as a mixing container, the transition of strands often occurs during the transcription of CoV RNA.
Complemented and highly homologous subgenomics can undergo recombination to form a new CoV.
Natural recombination philogenetic evidence has been found in HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-OC43, as well as animal CoV such as SL-CoV bristles and CoV-HKU9 bat.
The interaction of viruses in relation to the transmission
In addition to the aforement aforementioned three viral factors, the interaction of the virus with host receptors is another major factor that influencing interspecies transmission.
Here, SARS-CoV recombination is considered a typical example, which also shows evidence of positive selection during interspesion transmission.
Based on a comparative analysis between human SARS-CoV isolates and ferobed, SARS-CoV is thought to experience rapid adaptations in various hosts, especially with mutations in RBD protein S.
In general, RBD protein S CoV interacts with cell receptors and intensively becomes the target of host antibody response.
In SARS-CoV, RBD is the 318th to 510th amino acid in the S1 fragment, which binds to human ACE2 and its choresceptors as a way to enter the virus.
RBD SARS-CoV has the ability to recognize the ACE2 receptors of various animals, which include bats, ferns, rats, and raccoon dogs, thus enabling the transmission of interspecies viruses.
In fact, only 6 amino acid residues were observed differed between human viral isolates and ferocious in RBD and 4 of them were located in a receptor-binding motive for interaction with ACE2 receptors.
Wrought SARS-CoV has a K479N and S487T mutities in its RBD, which may increase the affinity of bulge protein interactions with human ACE2 receptors.
In other words, the two substitutions of these amino acids may be very important for the adaptation of the virus to humans.
It should be noted that SARS-CoV-2 has the same cell receptor as SARS-CoV.
The 30% difference between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV in the S1 protein S unit implicating the affinity to bind S protein with ACE2 is likely to have changed.
In fact, a cryo-EM study shows the affinity to bind human ACE2 and S SARS-CoV-2 proteins 10 to 20 times higher than the affinity between human ACE2 and SARS-CoV proteins.
It is also very important to know is whether there are other correspondors that might be needed for SARS-CoV-2 transmission.
Attractive thing, HCOV-NL63 also binds to ACE2, but with a different part on S.
There are many other HCoV receptors, such as aminopeptidase N for HCoV-229E and 9-O-teratized silic acid for HCoV-OC43.
These Receptors may also play a role in the success of these CoV adaptations in humans after the interspecies transmission of their animal hosts.
In addition to cell receptors, the end-of-device-induced HCoV transmission results are also governed by dependency factors and other host restrictions.
The difference of this host protein between humans and natural reservoir hosts, such as bats, Arabic camels, and rodents can be a hindrance for interspecies transmission.
HCOV must take over the host's dependency factor and divulge host restriction factors in order to be successful in interspecies transmission.
In this case, the molecular determinant in this important field of virus-device interaction should still be identified and characterised.
Shalmination of the entire genome is unbiased to look at the dependency factors and restrictions on SARS-CoV-2 hosts using the most advanced CRISPR technology may be beneficial.
The emergence of new HCoV: back to the beginning of the outbreak
The bat's CoV disquisability provides a great opportunity for the emergence of a new HCoV.
In this case, the bat's CoV serves as an HCoV gene ouster.
In addition, rapid mutations and genetic recombinations also move the evolution of HCoV and serve as two important steps in this process.
For example, the acquisition or loss of a new protein-casing gene has the potential to drastically modify the viral phenotype.
Among SARS-CoV accessory proteins, ORF8 is thought to play an important role in adapting to humans due to an insolated relative-SARS-CoV bat virus was found to encode a variety of ORF8 proteins.
SARS-CoV with the hallmark of 29 nucleotide removal is found in a strain isolated at the beginning of a human epidemic.
This deletion separates ORF8 to ORF8a and ORF8b and is thought to be an adaptive mutation that supports the host's transition.
In addition, SARS-CoV may have a recombination history with alpha-CoV and gamma-CoV hereditary, with a large number of smaller recombinant regions identified in RNA polymerases that depend on RNA.
Recombination locations are also identified in nsp9, mostly nsp10, and partly nsp14.
Similarly, recombination events between various lineages were shown to take place in the MERS-CoV epidemic, which took place in an Arab camel in Saudi Arabia.
In addition to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, recombination events have also been observed in other HCoV where HCoV underwent recombination with other animal CoV in its non-structural genes.
It should also be wary that artificial selection can play a role in accidental changes in the viral genome, most likely due to the release of the virus from the selection pressure experienced, such as by the host immune system.
An example of this effect is the complete loss of ORF4 on HCoV-229E prototyprotograph strains as a result of two nucleotides.
While intact ORF4 can be observable in bat and camel viruses recited with HCoV-229E, alfa-CoV alpaka indicates a single nucleotide insert that results in a shift in the skeleton.
Lastly, the new HCoV evolution is also driven by selection pressure in its reservoir hosts.
Mild or asymptomatic-negic symptoms are detected when the bat is infected with the CoV, which indicates interagation between the CoV and the bat.
It seems that the bat adapts well to CoV anatomically and physiologically.
For example, a defect in the activation of the pro-inflammatory response in the bat will reduce CoV-produced pathologies efficiently.
In addition, the activity of natural killer cells in the bat is suppressed due to the increase in the regulation of NKG2/CD94 natural killer cell receptors and the low level of molecular expression of class I main histocompatibility complex.
In addition, high levels of reactive (ROS (ROS) types of high bat metabolic activity can suppress CoV reimplication and affect correction by exoribonuclease so as to provide selection pressure for the manufacture of highly pathogenic viral strains when entering into a new host.
More pathogenic Strain CoV may also develop through recombination, which results in the acquisition of proteins or new protein features for host adaptation.
Therefore, it is not a coincidence that three new HCoVs have surfaced in the last two decades.
CoV is nonpathogenic or causes mild symptoms in its reservoir hosts, such as bats and camels.
The CoV is replicated strongly without triggering a strong host's immune response.
This is where there is the secret that there is an unsymptomatic carry-and-featured and the cause of severe cases in human infection.
Severe symptoms are mainly due to overactivation of immune responses as well as cytokine storms, with increasingly severe lung damage if the immune response gets stronger.
Conversely, in asymptomless carriers, the immune response has been detached from CoV replication.
Equal strategies to decide the linkage of the immune response may be beneficial in anti-SARS-CoV-2 therapies.
Interferon's response is especially strong on the bat.
Therefore, the administration of interferon type I, at least in the initial phase of SARS-CoV-2 infection in humans, should be beneficial.
In addition, activating inflamasom NLRP3 is defective.
For this reason, inflamasom NLRP3 inhibition with MCC950 may be beneficial in the handling of COVID-19.
The Emergence of SARS-CoV-2 follows the common groove of SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
While bat beta-CoV that shares 95% nucleotide homology with SARS-CoV has been found, there is also a bat CoV that shares 96% homology of nucleotides with SARS-CoV-2.
Although weasles and other animals in the market were found to carry a virus that is synonymous with SARS-CoV, direct intermediaries for SARS-CoV-2 have not been found.
A highly homologly homologous pangolin beta-CoV with SARS-CoV-2 has been found, indicating that pangolins may serve as one of the intermediaries or beta-CoV pangolins may contribute gene fragments in the late SARS-CoV-2 version.
Although still inquisitive, there is no evidence that SARS-CoV-2 is made by humans either intentionally or unintentionally.
The CoV is back in the limelight due to recent SARS-CoV-2 outbreak.
CoV studies in bats and other animals have profoundly changed our perception of the importance of zoonotic origins and animal reservoirs for HCoV on human transmission.
Very much the evidence shows that SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV, and SARS-CoV-2 are derived from bats and are transmitted to humans through intermediary hosts.
Given that SARS-CoV infection stems from contact between humans and ferocious ferns in a wet market, it may close the wet markets and the killing of all ferocious in them that ended the SARS epidemic on an effective basis.
For the same reason, pangolins were supposed to be removed from wet markets to prevent zoonotic transmissions, given the discovery of several pango panning beta-coV lineages close to SARS-CoV-2.
However, SARS-CoV-2 truth and mechanism are transmitted to humans through pangolins and other mammals must still be clarified in future studies.
On the other hand, MERS-CoV has long been in an Arabian camel.
This camel serves as an important means of transportation as well as the main source for meat, milk, skin, and wool products for the local community.
Arab camels are widespread throughout the Middle East and Africa.
Therefore, we are not likely to kill all the camels to control MERS, as was done in wild animal markets in China to prevent the spread of SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2.
In order to halt MERS outbreaks, a comprehensive approach must be taken to develop an effective camel vaccine against MERS-CoV and combined with other infection-control measures.
Before we manage to get rid of this virus, a new genotype can appear and cause an outbreak.
Various zoonotic CoVs are circulating in the wild.
In particular, potentially zoonotic CoV bats is very diverse.
There's a lot of possibilities for this zoonotic CoV to evolve and experience recombination, resulting in the emergence of new CoVs that are easier to transmit and/or more deadly to humans in the future.
The culture of eating wild animals in some places in China must be abandoned to reduce unnecessary contact between humans and animals.
With bitter experiences SARS, MERS, and COVID-19, preparations and better countermeasure plans should be implemented.
In fact, a lot of viruses have existed on this planet since time immemorial.
These viruses are located in their natural reservoir until there is an opportunity for deployment.
Although the bat has many characteristics that support the spread of the virus, the chances of humans in contact with bats and other wildlife species can be minimized if people are educated to stay away from wild animals.
Continuous surveillance of mammals needs to be done to better understand the ecology of CoV and its natural hosts, which will be shown to be beneficial in preventing incomparable animal-in-human transmissions and future outbreaks.
In conclusion, the most effective way to prevent viral zoonosis is that humans must maintain a distance from the ecological resimilar ecological reservoir of zoonotic viruses.
Some parts puzzles of SARS-CoV-2 zoonotic origin have not yet been found.
Firstly, if the bat transmits SARS-CoV-2 an ancestral virus to a pangolin, conditions when bats and pangolins sharing ecological niches are important things to know.
Second, if the bat has a more direct role in the transmission to humans, the way humans in contact with bats must be determined.
Third, if a third mamm mamm mamm mamm mamm mammal acts as an intermediary host, the way ma mamm mamm mammal it interacts with other species, which includes humans, bats and pangolins must be clarified.
Finally, because many mammals, including pets that may be susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, both trial and surveillance infections should be carried out.
Either the bat, pangolins, or other mammals, it is expected that SARS-CoV-2 or its almost identical parent virus can be identified in its natural hosts in the foreseeable world.
Continuous investigations in this area will outline the evolutionary path of SARS-CoV-2 in animals, with important implications in the prevention and control of COVID-19 in humans.
Renovation of diagnostic criterion "suspected cases" and COVID-19 "confirmed cases" needs to be carried out
As of February 6, 2020, our team publicized a quick advice guide for the diagnosis and treatment of new coronavirus infections 2019 (2019-nCoV). This guide is based on our experience and becomes a good reference to fight pandemics around the world.
However, 2019 coronavirus disease (COVID-19) is a new disease, our awareness and knowledge is increasing based on ongoing research findings and clinical practical experiences; therefore, diagnosis and copulation strategies are also constantly being renewed.
In this letter, we respond to one of the comments in our guidance and provides the latest diagnostic criteria for “suspected cases” and “confirmed cases” based on Diagnosis and Handling Guidance for COVID-19 (seven versions) issued by the National Health Commission of the People’s Republic of China.
In December 2019, the new coronavirus 2019 (2019-nCoV) has led to an outbreak, which is currently formally referred to as 2019 coronavirus (COVID-19) and the virus was named Coronavirus High-Heavy Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
On March 11, in 2020, WHO described COVID-19 as a pandemic.
To fight SARS-CoV-2 infection, our team is developing a quick advice guide and the guidance has been published online at Military Medical Research on February 6, 2020.
Such guidance has attracted a lot of attention since it was published.
However, it should be noted that COVID-19 is a new disease, our awareness and knowledge are increasing based on ongoing research findings as well as clinical practical experience; therefore, diagnosis and copulation strategies are also constantly being renewed.
As an example of a Diagnosis and Handling Guidance for COVID-19 issued by the National Health Commission of the People's Republic of China (http://www.nhk.gov.cn/), between January 16, 2020 to March 3, 2020, this commission has issued a total of seven editions with a part of the context has been changed quite a lot.
Our guidance received comments from Zhou et al., they introduced a simple score-absording proposal based on their clinical experience.
Result their work adds new evidence for our guidance as well as being a valuable reference to this pandemic all over the world.
We support the results of their significant work and express our gratitude.
However, the results of their work also require an update based on Diagnosis and Handling Guidance for recent COVID-19 (several versions of trials) and recent studies.
Based on the seventh edition (3 March 2020), to confirm an alleged case required a combination of one picture of an epidemiological history with two clinical manifestations to create a comprehensive analysis, or the fulfillment of three clinical manifestations if there is no clear epidemiological narration:
Epidemiological history: (1) travel history or living in the city of Wuhan and the surrounding area, or any other community where COVID-19 cases have been reported in the last 14 days prior to the onset of symptoms; (2) a history of contact with contagious cases SARS-CoV-2 (with positive nucleic acid tests); (3) a history of contact with patients experiencing fevers or respiratory symptoms from the city of Wuhan and the surrounding area, or other communities in which COVID-19 cases have been reported in the last 14 days prior to the symptoms.
Clinical manifestations: (1) fevers and/or respiratory symptoms; (2) imaging features of COVID-19 infection; (3) the total count of white blood cells indicates normal lymphocyte count, decreases, or decreases in the initial on-circution stages.
Diagnosing confirmed cases should be based on suspected cases with any of the following pathogenic or serological evidence as follows: ((1) positive real-time PCR test for SARS-CoV-2; (2) sequation of the entire genome of the virus shows high homogeneity for known new coronaviruses; (3) positive for specific IgM antibody and IgG antibodies against SARS-CoV-2 in the serum test;
We can see that the real-time nucleic acid PCR test in a sample of the respiratory or blood canal is added in the second (January 18, 2020) and third (January 22, 2020).
The pathogenic detection in the blood sample was added in the fourth (January 27, 2020) and fifth (February 8, 2020); then, serological evidence was added to the seventh edition.
These modifications are based on the researchers' ongoing research to look for optimal nucleic acid detection kits for a quick diagnosis, as well as samples from the airway including blood retrieVation, which increases the availability of a variety of specimens, and supports the inclusion of certain antibody-possitive results into confirmed criteria.
In addition, there's an increasing amount of evidence that reminds us to be wary of patients with atypical and asympthy symptoms.
Because of this reason, Zhou et al. flow charts should be updated because they classify people without clinical symptoms as “low risk”.
The scoring system also needs to be verifiable in future clinical practices and clinical studies.
In conclusion, we hope more direct evidence will appear and ask the reader to provide a comment.
For diagnosis of "predicted cases" and "confirmed cases", we advise to follow and comply with the latest guidelines in their respective countries.
Our team will also update our guidance periodically as an aid.
Bangladesh reports five new deaths resulting from COVID-19, highest daily death toll
Yesterday, Bangladesh confirmed five new deaths due to COVID-19 that day.
It's the highest death toll in a day due to the virus.
Until yesterday, the Institute of Epidemiology, Disease Control and Research (IEDCR) of Bangladesh reported the recorded number of infectious cases covering 114 active cases and 33 cured cases living at home.
Recorded a total of 17 deaths.
In an online news equanim, IEDCR director Dr Meerjady Sabrina Flora said the deaths included four men and a woman.
According to Dr. Meerjady, two cases are over the age of 60, two cases between 51 and 60 years, and one case between 41-50.
He also said the two victims belonged to Dhaka.
On March 11, 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 as a pandemic.
A hospital official informed Anadolu Agency, a local news agency, that one who died was Jalal Saifur Rahman, director of the Bengali Anti-Corruption Commission, who was being treated at Maitre's Kuwaiti Hospital.
