If you have a confidential or sensitive issue, please send an email to Bryan Judan.
Do not let any of these changes be seen as the waiver of our work and obligations.
Conversely, this change is an acknowletion that, at present, our work and liabilities may need to be adapted in ways we have never done before.
These changes are the steps that we believe are important to support each other in this situation so that we can continue to work, provide the necessary support our movements, and provide the world of services they rely on.
The work that we planned in advance will be there waiting for us when the time has arrived.
For now, here's the time to support each other and create spaces for important jobs that will come in a few weeks and maybe the next few months.
We require all of you to make it happen, for that, we want you to take care of yourself and your family so that you can work your best when needed.
Finally, please wash your hands and don’t touch your face!
Katherine, CRT (Amanda K, Amy V, Bryan J, Doren D, Gregory V, Jaime V, Jaime V, Joel L, Lynette L, Ryan, and Tony S), and other Leadership Team members (Grant I, Heather W, Jaime V, Janeen U, Lisa S, Robyn A, Ryan M, and Toby N).
angiotensin-alter modifying enzyme 2 (ACE2) is an enzyme that is attached to the outer surface of the cells (cell membranes) in the lungs, arteries, heart, kidneys, and intestines.
ACE2 has the opposite activity of angiotensin-conversing enzyme (ACE), by reducing the amount of angiotensin-II and increasing Angiotensin (1-7), thus making it a promising drug target to treat cardiovascular disease. ACE2 also serves as an entry point into the cell for some coronavirus.
ACE2 the human version is often referred to as hACE2.
Angiotentic-vesorting enzymes 2 are metaloenzymes containing zinc located on the surface of endothelial cells and other cells.
The ACE2 protein has an M2 peptide domain terminal-N and an amino renal collectrin amino acid transporter terminal-C domain.
ACE2 is a single-taundry membrane protein type I, with an enzymatically active domain being exposed to the surfaces of cells in the pulmonary and other tissues.
The ACE2's extracellaneous domain is separated from the transmembrane domain by other enzymes, known as sheddase, and the resulting dissolved protein is released into the bloodstream and eventually excreted into the urine.
ACE2 exists in most organs: ACE2 is attached to the cell membranes mainly pulmonary alveolar cells of type II, small intestinal enterocytes, endothelial and venous cells, and arterial smooth muscle cells in most organs.
MRNA ACE2 expressions are also found in cerebral cortex, striatum, hypothalamus, and brainstelomb.
The main function of ACE2 is as an ACE equalizer.
ACE decomplicates angiotensin hormone I into angiotensin II vasoconstrixation.
Finally, ACE2 chops the amino acid phanylalanine the carboxyl terminal of angiotensin II (Asp-Arg-Val-Tyr-His-Pro-Phe) and hydrolyze it into angiotensin (1--7) vasodilator (H-asp-Tyr-Ile-His-Pro-OH).
ACE2 can also break down a number of other peptides including [des-Arg9] -bradicin, applein, neurotensin, dinorphine A, and ghrelin.
ACE2 also regulates the transporter membrane traffic of SLC6A19-neutral amino acids and is involved in Hartnup disease.
As a transmembrane protein, ACE2 serves as the main entry point into the cell for some coronavirus, which includes HCOV-NL63; SARS-CoV (the virus that causes SARS), and SARS-CoV-2 (a virus that causes COVID-19).
More specifically, the binding of S1 proteins on spikes or protrusion SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV2 with ACE2 enzynastic domains on the cell's surface result in endocytosis and trajectorization of viruses and enzymes into endosomes located within the cell.
The incoming process of this virus also requires primming protein S by serin protease host TMPRS2, whose inhibition is currently underinvestigated as a potential therapy. This fact makes people hypothesize that a decrease in ACE2 levels in the cell may be able to help combat the infection.
However, many professional communities and regulatory bodies recommend continuing standard ACE and ARB inhibitor therapy.
A systematic review and metaanalysis, published on July 11, 2012, found that "the use of ACE inhibitors led to a significant 34% reduction in the risk of pneumonia compared to control."
Subsequently, "the use of ACE inhibitors also lowers the risk of pneumonia in patients who have a higher risk for pneumonia, especially patients with stroke and heart failure.
The use of ACE inhibitors is also associated with a decrease in pneumonia-related mortality, although the results are less strong compared to the risk of overall pneumonia.
Recombinant human ACE2 (rHACE2) is expected to be a new therapy for acute pulmonary injury, and appears to improve pulmonary hemodynamics and oxygen saturation in swines with acute respiratory syndrome induced by lipolysaccharides.
Participation rhACE2 in humans is about 10 hours with a working turnover of 30 minutes in addition to 24-acrifice (duration).
Some findings show that rhACE2 may be a promissive drug for patients with an intolerance of the classic renin-angiotensin (Ras inhibition) system (RAS inhibitors) or in diseases with angiotensin II in increased blood circulturation. Infusion rhACE2 has been evaluated in clinical trials for the treatment of acute respiratory distress syndrome.
The COVID-19 application is a mobile software application designed to aid contact trajectorization to overcome the 2019-2020 coronavirus pandemic, the process of identifying people ("contacts") who may have been in contact with an infected person.
Many applications are developed or prospiced, which have the official support of the government in several regions and jurisdictions.
Several work frameworks for building contact tracing applications have been developed.
Privacy issues are questionable, especially with regard to systems that are based on tracking the geographic location of app users.
Alternatives that don’t particularly infiltrate privacy include the use of the Bluetooth signal to record the user’s closeness to other phones.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple jointly announced that they will integrate the functionality to support such Bluetooth-based apps directly into Android and iOS operating systems.
In China, the Chinese government, along with Alipay, has deployed an application that allows citizens to check if they have come into contact with COVID-19 sufferers.
This application is used in more than 200 Chinese cities. In Singapore, an application called TraceTogether is used.
This application was developed by the local IT community, was released as an open source and will be handed over to the government. North Makedonia launched "StopKorona!", a Bluetooth-based application to track exposures against potentially infected people and provide a quick response to health care authorities.
This application was developed by the Ministry of Communication and Technology and the Ministry of Health.
Until April 14, 2020, the application is still waiting for the approval of the Google Play Store and the Apple App Store.
On April 12, the government stated that contact trajectorizing apps were in an advanced development phase and it would be available for dissemination within a number of weeks. Similar applications were planned in Ireland and in France ("StopCovid").
Australia and New Zealand are considering an application based on Singapore's TraceTogether application and BlueTrace protocol. Russia intends to introduce a geofencing app for COVID-19-diagnosed patients living in Moscow; this app is designed to make sure they don't leave their homes.
Ross Anderson, professor of security engineering at the University of Cambridge, mentioned a number of practical problems that may occur in application-based systems, which include false positives and a possible lack of efficiency if application-pictured data is limited to a small portion of the population alone.
To address concerns about the dissemination of mislead or harmful "coronavirus" apps, Apple sets limits on the type of organization that can add coronavirus-related applications to the App Store, i.e. limiting only to "eofficial" or reputable organizations.
Google and Amazon implemented similar restrictions.
Proponents of privacy are voicing their concerns regarding the implications of mass surveillance using the coronavirus app, in particular regarding whether surveillance infrastructure created to deal with the coronavirus pandemic will be halted after threats have passed.
Amnesty International and more than 100 other organizations are issuing statements calling for restrictions on this kind of surveillance.
This organization declares eight conditions on government projects:
surveillance must be "appropriate legal, necessary, and proportionate".
Explanation of monitoring and surveillance should have a sunset clause;
the use of the data should be limited for COVID-19 purposes;
the security and anonymity of the data must be protected and proven to be protected on the basis of evidence;
digital surveillance should avoid the worsening of discrimination and marginalization;
any division of data with a third party must be defined in the law.
there must be a protection against the abuse and the right of citizens to respond to abuse.
Participation means all "relevant snipants of interest" are required, which include public health experts and marginalized groups. The German Chaos Computer Club (CCC) and Reporters Without Borders (Reporter ohne Grenzen) (RSF) also issued a checklist.
Proposed Google/Apple's proposed plan intends to address surveillance issues continuously by deleting the tracking mechanism from the device's operating system after it is no longer needed.
Some countries use network-based location trajectorization, and non-appropriate, thus eliminating the need to download apps and the ability to avoid tracking.
In Israel, network-based tracking is approved.
Network-based solutions that have access to raw location data have the potential for significant privacy issues.
However, not all systems with central servers need to access personal location data; a number of privacy-protecting systems have been created and use central servers only for inter-communication (see section below).
In South Korea, non-approach-based systems are used to carry out contact trajectory.
In exchange for the use of a dedicated application, the system collects tracking information from a variety of sources, including mobile device tracking data and card transaction data, and combining this information to generate notifications via text messages to potentially infected people.
In addition to the use of this information to warn potential contacts, the government also makes the location information available to the public, which is permitted with widespread changes to information privacy laws after the outbreak of MERS in the country.
This information is available to the public through a number of applications and websites. Countries such as Germany are considering using a centralized system as well as a system that protects privacy.
Until April 6, 2020, the details have not been released.
The tracking of privacy-protecting contacts is an already established concept, with a large number of research literature ranging from at least 2013.On April 7, 2020, more than a dozen groups of experts worked on privacy-friendly solutions, such as using Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE), to record the proximity of users to other phones.
However, PEPP-PT is coordinating efforts that contain a centralized and decentralized approach, and not a single protocol. Decentralized protocols include Proximity Tracking Proximity that Protects Decentralized Privacy (DP-PPT/DP-3T), Temporary Contact Number (TCN, formerly referred to as Contact Event Number, CEN), Protocol and Privacy Sensitive Mechanism for Mobile Contact Tracking (PACT), and others.
In this protocol, personally identifiable personal data never leaves the device, and all matches occur on the device.
The Privacy Group at MIT Media Lab is developing SafePaths, a platform to use techniques that protect privacy when collecting and utilising locations or groove intersecting data to track COVID-19 dissemination.
It's based on research from the white sheet "Apps Gone Rogue: Maintaining Personal Privacy in an Epidemic" which was released in March 2020.
SafeTrace uses secure hardware technology to allow users to share locations and sensitive health data with other users and clerics without sacrificing such data privacy.
As of April 5, 2020, the global TCN Coalition was established by groups that have essentially the same approach and is an overlapping protocol, with the aim of easing fragmentation and enabling global interoperability for tracking and warning applications, which is a key aspect of achieving widespread acceptance.
On April 9, in 2020, the Singapore government announced that it had made the BlueTrace protocol used by its government's official application as an open source.
As of April 10, 2020, Google and Apple, the company that controls Android and iOS mobile platforms, announced an initiative for contact trajectory, which they claim will protect privacy, according to a combination of Bluetooth Low Energy and cryptographic technology that protects privacy.
They've also published the core technological specifications used in those systems.
According according to Apple and Google, the system is intended to be launched in three stages:
launch tool to allow the government to create an official coronavirus tracking app that protects privacy
Integration of this function directly to Google's iOS and Android and Apple plans to address data retrieval and surveillance issues continuously by initially distributing the system through operating system updates, then removing them in the same way after threats have passed.
b'Respiracy of the drug (also referred to as the provision of a new purpose, a new profile, or a new task in the drug or the transition of therapy) is the use of drugs that have been approved for new medical purposes, i.e. for diseases or medical conditions that are different from the initial goal of drug development.
This pathway is a scientific research path that is currently being carried out to develop safe and effective COVID-19 treatment.
The direction of other research covers the development of COVID-19 vaccines and convalescent plasma transfusions. SARS-CoV-2 has about 66 proteins that can target drugs, each protein has several ligand binding sites.
An analysis of such ligand-binding sites can be the basis of an effective antiviral drug development project against COVID-19 proteins.
Some of SARS-CoV-2 most important target proteins are papain-like proteases, reconnaissance RNA, helicases, S proteins, and ADP ribophosphate.
Hud Hussein A, et al examined several candidate compounds that were later optimized and analysed the resemblance similarities with the drugs that had been approved and had the highest presimilar to speed up the development of potent anti-SARS-CoV-2 drugs in its preclinical studies to be recommended in the design of clinical studies.
Chlolokuin is an antimalarial drug that is also used against several autoimmune diseases.
On March 18, the WHO announced that chloroquine and hydrochlorokuin are two of the four drugs to be researched as part of a Solidarity clinical trial.
New York Governor Andrew Cuomo, announced that the chloroquine and hydrochlorocucine trials in New York will begin on March 24. On March 28, the FDA authorised the use of hydroxychlorochuin sulfate and chloroquine phosphate based on Emergencies (EUA).
This treatment has not yet been sanctioned by the FDA's clinical trial process and is authoritized based on EUA only as an experimental treatment for in-preserved patients, but cannot receive treatment in clinical trials.
The CDC states that "use, dosage, or duration of hydroxychlorochuin for prophylaxis or treatment of SARS-CoV-2 infection" has not been established.
Doctors said they used the drug when "there is no other option".
A team of Turkish researchers in Istanbul is doing a small study on the use of chloroquine combined with zinc, vitamin A, vitamin C, and vitamin D.
Large studies are taking place at Duke University and the University of Oxford.
NYU Langone Medical School is undergoing safety and efficacious testing of hydroxycloroccine preventive use.
China's clinical trials in Wuhan and Shenzhen claim that favipiravir proved to be "clear effective".
Thity-fifty-five patients in Shenzhen were negatively tested in a 4-day median, while the duration of the disease was 11 days in 45 patients who did not receive the drug.
In a study carried out in Wuhan in 240 patients with pneumonia, half of the number of patients were given favipiravir and the other half received umphyenovir.
Italy's Pharmacy Agency reminds the public that the existing evidence to support the drug is not much and too early.
On April 2, Germany announced it would buy the drug from Japan for supplies and use the military to deliver the medicine to the university's hospital, where the drug will be used to treat COVID-19 patients.
According to the South China Morning Post, Shinzo Abe has made an offer to the Trump administration on the purchase of the drug. The drug may be less effective in the case of a severe disease with a virus that has multiplied.
This drug may not be safe if it is used by pregnant women or people who are trying to conceive.
A study on lopinavir/ritonavir (Kaletra), which is a combination of lopinavir and ritonavir antiquities, concluding that "there are no observed benefits".
This drug is designed to inhibit the replication of HIV by binding to protease.
A team of researchers at the University of Colorado is trying to modify the drug to find a compound that will bind to SARS-CoV-2 protease.
WHO included lopinavir/ritonavir in international Solidarity trials.
Remdesivir was made and developed by Gilead Sciences as a treatment for Ebola virus disease and Marburg virus infection. Later, Gilead Sciences found that Remdesivir had antiviral activity vitro against several filo-, pneumo-, paramikso-, and coronavirus.
One problem with antiviral treatment is the occurrence of resistance through mutations that can lead to more severe disease and transmission.
Several pre-earning pre-trial studies show remdesivir may have high genetic barriers to resistance. There are several ongoing clinical trials, including two clinical trials conducted by the University of Cleveland Hospital, namely one for people with moderate illness and another for people who are terminally ill.
There are three ongoing clinical trials for intravenous vitamin C for people who are hospitalized and severe COVID-19 sick, namely two controlled placebo (China, Canada) and one without control (Italy).
The New York State began trials for azithromycin antibiotics on March 24, 2020.
Japan's National Center for Global Health and Medicine (NCGM) is planning clinical trials for Alvesco (ciclesonide) Teijin, corticosteroid inhalation for asthma, for the treatment of new presymptomatic coronavirus infection patients.
For an angiotensin-conversing enzymes, Phase II trials are taking place with 200 patients will be recruited from severe cases hospitalized in Denmark, Germany, and Austria to find out the effectiveness of treatment.
Researchers from the Montreal Heart Institute in Canada are currently scrutining the Role of Coccine in Reducing inflammatory and pulmonary Complications in Patients Suspecting Mild COVID-19.
The study, named COLCORONA, recruited 6000 adults aged 40 and older who were diagnosed with COVID-19 and experienced mild symptoms so it didn't require hospitalization.
Women who are pregnant or lactating or do not use effective con contraceptive methods do not qualifies.
Several anticoagulants are being tested in Italy.
Low-lecular heparins are widely used to treat patients so as to impolete the Italian Drug Agency to publish guidelines on their use.
A multi-centralized study in 300 patients who examined the use of sodium enosaparin at prophylactic and therapeutic doses was announced in Italy on April 14.
Because SARS-CoV-2 is a virus, a lot of scientific attention is focused on providing new medical purposes on antiviral drugs approved and developed for previous outbreaks, such as MERS, SARS, and West Nile virus.
Ribavirin : ribavirin is recommended for COVID-19 treatment according to 7th-mentioned Chinese guidelines
Umifenovir: umphyenovir is recommended for COVID-19 treatment according to 7th-mentioned Chinese guidelines
Some identified antibiotics could potentially be a drug that can be given a new purpose for COVID-19 treatment:
Tocilizumab (Anti-Receptor IL-6): Approved by China.
In addition, trials were also carried out in Italy and China, see also Tocilizumab #COVID-19.
COVID-19 Vaccine is a hypothetic vaccine against coronavirus 2019 (COVID-19) coronavirus disease (COVID-19).
While there is not yet a vaccine that completes clinical trials, there have been a lot of efforts being made to develop the vaccine.
At the end of February 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) said that, according to WHO, the vaccine against SARS-CoV-2, causative of this disease, will not be available in less than 18 months.
Five vaccination candidates were in Phase I safety studies in April.
COVID-19 was identified in December 2019.
Large outbreaks spread around the world in 2020, which resulted in large investments and research activities to develop vaccines.
A lot of organizations use published genomes to develop vaccines that could possibly fight SARS-CoV-2.
CEPI's iniditivity gave a statement in April that what must be observed in developing the vaccine is speed, production capacity, deployment on the required scale, as well as global access.
In April, CEPI scientists reported that 10 different technology platforms were in research and development during the beginning of 2020 to create an effective vaccine against COVID-19.
The main target platforms that advance to Phase I's security studies, include:
nucleic acids (DNA and RNA) (phase I disseminers and vaccinate candidates: Moderna, mRNA-1273)
Viral Vector (Diversity of Phase I and vaccine candidates: CanSino Biologics, adenovirus vector type 5)
As reported by CEPI scientists in April, a total of 115 vaccupational nominees are in the early stages of development, with 78 confirmed as an active project (79, according to the Milken Institute), and 37 others announced, but with little public information available (conceived to be on-destruction or being designed).
The I-II phase trial conducts initial safety and immunogenicity tests, is usually done randomly, plasebo-controlled, and in some locations, while determining a more precise effective dose.
Phase III trials usually involve more participants, including control groups, and test the effectiveness of the vaccine to prevent the disease, while monitoring side effects at optimal doses.
Of the 79 vaccine candidates who are actively being developed (confirmed in early April 2020), 74 of them have not yet been in human evaluation (still in "praclinical" research).
On January 24, 2020 in Australia, the University of Queensland announced that it is investigating a potential molecular clamping vaccine that will genetically modify viral proteins to stimulate an immune reaction.
On January 24, 2020 in Canada, the International Vaccine Center (VIDO-InterVac) at Saskatchewan University announced that it began developing vaccines, with testing targets in humans in 2021.
Vaccine-development project was announced at China's Center for Disease Control and Prevention on January 26, 2020 and Hong Kong University on January 28.
On January 29, 2020, Janssen Pharmaceutical Company, led by Hanneke Schuitemaker, announced that they began developing vaccines.
Janssen is cooperating with his biotechnological partner, Vaxart, to develop an oral vaccine.
On March 18, 2020, Emergent BioSolutions announced a production partnership with Vaxart to develop the vaccine.
On February 8, 2020, OncoGen's laboratory in Romania published a paper on the design of vaccines with technologies similar to the technology used for cancer neoantigen vaccination therapy.
On March 25, the head of the research agency announced that it had completed the synthesis of the vaccine and would begin testing.
On February 27, 2020, Generex's subsidiaries, NuGenerex Immuno-Oncology, announced they were embarking on a vaccinious project to create an Ii-Key peptide vaccine against COVID-19.
They want to produce vaccine candidates that can be tested in humans "within 90 days."
On March 5, 2020, the University of Washington in St. Louis announced its project to develop the vaccine.
As of March 5, 2020, the U.S. Army's Medical and Supply Research Command at Fort Detrick and Walter Reed Army's Research Institute in Silver Spring, both in western Maryland, announced that they were developing a vaccine.
On March 10, 2020, Emergent Biosolutions announced that they are cooperating with Novavax Inc.
in the development and manufacture of vaccines.
Subsequently, this partnership announced a preclinical testing plan and Phase I clinical trials in July 2020.
On March 12, 2020, India's Ministry of Health announced they were working on 11 isolates, and even on a fast-lane, the development of the vaccine took at least about one and a half to two years.
On March 12, 2020, Medicago, a biotechnology company in Quebec City, Quebec, reported the development of coronavirus-like particles with partial funding from the Canadian Institutes for Health Research.
Candidates of this vaccine are in laboratory research, with human testing planned in July or August of 2020.
Earlier that week, The Guardian reported that U.S. President, Donald Trump, had offered a "bill amount of money for exclusive access to the Covid-19 vaccine" to CureVac, who was protested by the German government.
On March 17, 2020, American pharmaceutical company Pfizer announced a partnership with the German company, BioNTech, to co-develop an mRNA-based vaccine.
At the moment, mRNA-based vaccination candidate BNT162, is in preclinical testing with clinical trials it is expected to begin in April 2020.
On March 17, 2020 in Italy's Italian biotech company Takis Biotech announced they will get preclinical testing results in April 2020 and vaccine candidates residing in the late stages can begin to be tested on humans in the fall.
On March 19, 2020 in France, the Epidemic Preparation Innovation Coalition (CEPI) announced an investment of US$4.9.9 million in a COVID-19 vaccine research consortium involving Pasteur, Themis Bioscience (Wina, Austria), and the University of Pittsburgh, with CEPI's total investment in COVID-19 vaccine development reaching US$29 million.
Other CEPI investment partners for COVID-19 vaccinated development are Moderna, Curevac, Inovio, Novavax, University of Hong Kong, University of Oxford, and University of Queensland.
On March 20, 2020, Russian health officials announced that scientists had begun testing six vaccine candidates on animals.
Eclipse researcher London announced on March 20, 2020 that it is developing an RNA vaccine that can amplify itself for COVID-19.
Vaccine cadidates were developed in 14 days after receiving sequens from China.
At the end of mid-March, the Canadian government announced a $275-million-funding fund for 96 medical countermeasures research projects against COVID-19, which includes a number of vaccine candidates at Canadian companies and universities, such as Medicago and Saskatchewan University initiatives.
At about the same time, the Canadian government announced a special C$192 million fund to develop the COVID-19 vaccine, with plans to set up a national "vaccine bank containing several new vaccines that can be used if other coronavirus outbreaks occur.
On April 2, 2020, researchers at the University of Pittsburgh School of Medicine reported PittCoVacc's testing, COVID-19 vaccination candidate, in mice; they stated that "AMNA who administered the SARS-CoV-2 S1 subunit vaccine elicit a potent antigen-specific antibody response [to mice] that began to be seen 2 weeks after immunization."
On April 16, 2020 in Canada, Waterloo University's Faculty of Pharmacy Announced the design of DNA-based vaccinating candidates as a potential nasal spray.
Using bacteriophage, DNA is designed to replicate in human bacterium to produce non-robial-fashion-meatile particles, which can stimulate the immune system to produce antibodies against SARS-CoV-2 viruses.
In March of 2020, U.S. government, industry, and three U.S. universities rallied resources to access supercompanities from IBM’s Hewlett Packard Enterprise, Amazon, and Google.
Some vaccinates have a heterologic effect, which is also called non-specific effects.
This means that these vaccines can have benefits outside the disease it prevents.
Further scrambled trials in Australia attempted to include 4,170 health workers.
There's a good chance a vaccine in development won't be as safe or effective.
Inititial research to assess the efficacy of vaccinates using specific COVID-19 veterinary models, such as ACE2-genetic mice, other laboratory animals, and non-human primates, demonstrating the need for damming measures to achieve 3 biological safety levels in dealing with living viruses, and international coordination to ensure standard safety procedures.
Vaccines against SARS and MERS have been tested on non-human animal models.
Until 2020, there has been no drug or protective vaccine for SARS proven to be safe and effective in humans.
According to a research paper published in 2005 and 2006, the identification and development of new vaccines and drugs to treat SARS is a priority for governments and public health agencies around the world. In addition, proven vaccines for MERS also do not yet exist.
When MERS becomes prevalent, existing SARS research is believed to be a useful templat to develop vaccines and therapies against MERS-CoV infections.
In March 2020, there was one MERS (DNA-flean-based) vaccine that completed phase I clinical trials in humans, and the other three vaccines were in the process, all of which were virus-vector vaccines, two adenovirus vectors (ChAdOx1-MERS, BVRS-GamVac), and one vector-vector (MVA-MERS-S).
Social media posts echo the conspiracist theories claiming the virus behind COVID-19 has been known before and that vaccines have been made available.
Patents quoted by various social media posts refer to existing patents for genetic sequences and vaccines for other coronavirus excavations, such as the SARS coronavirus.
b'Coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) is an infectious disease caused by coronavirus severe acute respiratory syndrome 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
Common symptoms include fever, cough, and shortness of breath.
Other symptoms can include fatigue, muscle pain, diarrhea, sore throat, loss of olfactory power, and abdominal pain.
The time from exposure to the onset of symptoms is generally about five days, but it can range from two to fourteen days.
Although most cases result in mild symptoms, some develop into viral pneumonia and maternity organ failure.
Until April 17, 2020, more than 2.24 million cases have been reported in 210 countries and territorial territory, resulting in more than 153,000 deaths.
More than 56,58,000 people have cured. The virus is mainly spread between humans when close contact occurs, often through small splashes or droplets produced through coughing, sneezing, or while speaking.
Although produced when exhaling, droplets usually fall to the ground or to the surface instead of contagious to long-distance.
People can also become infected by touching a contaminated surface and then touching the eyes, nose, or mouth.
On the surface, this virus can last up to 72 hours.
This virus is very contagious during the first three days after the onset of symptoms, although the spread may occur before the symptoms appear and after the next stage of the disease. The standard disbursement method is with a real-time reverse transcription (rRT-PCR) polymerase chain (rRT-PCR) reaction in nasopharyngeal swabs.
The use of masks is recommended for people who are suspected of contracting the virus and the person caring for it.
Recommendations regarding the use of masks by the general public vary; some authorities recommend not to use masks, some recommend their use, and others necessitate their use.
At the same time, there are no vaccines or antiviral treatments specifically for COVID-19.
Local transmission of the disease has been recorded in almost all countries in six WHO regions.
People who are infected with the virus can be without symptoms or experiencing symptoms such as the flu, such as fever, cough, fatigue, and shortness of breath.
Emergency symptoms include difficulty breathing, pain or tightness in the chest constantly, confusion, difficulty waking, and bluish-faithful face or lips; medical copulation is immediately recommended if these symptoms appear.
Upper respiratory symptoms, such as sneezing, smearing, or sore throat can be found, but are less common.
Gastroin gastrointestinal symptoms, such as nausea, vomiting, and diarrhoea, are observed in varying percentages.
Some cases in China were initially only with a clinical picture of chest tightness and palpitations.
In some people, the disease can develop into pneumonia, compound organ failure, and death.
This period is called incubation.
The incubation period for COVID-19 is usually five to six days, but it can range from two to 14 days.
97.5% of people who are symptomatic will experience symptoms within 11.5 days of the infection. The report shows that not everyone who is infected is experiencing symptoms.
The role of these asymptomatic-neicent carriers in transmission is not yet fully known; however, preliminary evidence shows that they may contribute to the spread of the disease.
At this time, the proportion of infected people who didn't show symptoms is not yet known and being researched, with the Korean Center for Disease Control and Prevention (KCDC) reporting that 20% of all confirmed cases remain unsymptomary during hospitalization.
China's National Health Commission began to include unsympific cases in its daily case on April 1, 166 infections on that day, 130 (70%) did not show symptoms at the time of testing.
Sputum and subliquid water can carry viral charges on a large scale.
Speakfully releases more sprinkles or droplets rather than talking normally.
A study in Singapore found that an unalcovered cough can cause droplets to be pushed for as far as 4.5 meters (15 feet).
Although this virus is not usually transmitted through the air, the United States Academy of National Science shows that bioaerosol transmission is likely to occur and air collectors placed in the hallway outside people's rooms produce positive samples for viral RNA.
Some medical procedures, such as pulmonary intubation and resuscitation (RJP), can make breathing secretions turn into an aerosol and then spread through the air.
Although there are concerns that the virus can spread through feces, this risk is believed to be low. The virus is most contagious when the person is symptomatic; although the spread may occur before symptoms appear, the risk is low.
The European Center for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC) said that although it is not yet fully clear how easily the disease is spreading, one person generally infects two to three others. The virus can survive on the surface for hours for days.
Notably, it is found that the virus can be detected for one day on cardboard / cardboard, up to three days on plastic (polypropylene) and stainless steel (AISI 304), and for up to four hours at 99% copper.
However, this varies depending on the humidity and temperature.
Soaps and detergents are also effective if used correctly; soap products decompose the protective fat layers of the virus, deactivate the virus, as well as exploquiate them from the skin and other surfaces.
Other dissolves, such as benzalkonium chloride and chlorhexidine gluconate (surgical disinfectants), are less effective. In a study in Hong Kong, saliva samples were taken in medians two days after hospitalization began.
In the five out of six patients, the first sample showed the highest viral charge, and the sixth patient showed the highest virility in second-day testing.
Koronavirus severe acute rectangular respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) is a new severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus, which was first illusitised out of three people with pneumonia associated with clusters of cases of acute respiratory illness in Wuhan.
All the characteristics of the new SARS-CoV-2 virus exist in the coronavirus that is related in nature.
Outside of the human body, the virus was killed by household soap, which damaged its protective bubbles. SARS-CoV-2 is closely related to first SARS-CoV.
Pulmonary is the organ most affected by COVID-19 because the virus accesses the host cell through angiotensin-alifying enzyme 2 (ACE2), which is most abundant in pulmonary alveolar cells.
This virus uses special surface glycoproteins so-called "spikes or bulges" (pepliers) to connect to ACE2 and enter host cells.
Accurate heart injury was found in 12% of infected people who are hospitalized in Wuhan, China, and are more common in severe illness.
Cardiovascular level symptoms are high due to systemic inflammatory responses and immune system disorders during the development of the disease, but acute myocardial injury may also be associated with ACE2 receptors in the heart.
ACE2 Receptors are expressed very strongly in the heart and are involved in heart function.
The incidence of thrombosis (31%) and high venous thromboembolism (25%) is found in COVID-19 infection patients in the ICU and may be associated with poor prognosis. Otopsies in people who die from COVID-19 find damage to alveolar diffus (DAD) and inflammatory infiltrates containing lymphocytes in the lungs.
Although SARS-COV-2 has tropisme for airway epithelial cells that express ACE2, severe COVID-19 patients have systemic hyperinflammatory symptoms.
In particular, T cells that excrete pathogenic GM-CSF are shown to be correlated with monocyte recruitment that excretes IL-6 inflammatories and severe pulmonary pathologies in COVID-19 patients.
Lymphocytic infiltrates have also been reported on the autopsy.
WHO has published several testing protocols for the disease.
The standard method of testing is a real-time (rRT-PCR transcription (rRT-PCR).
These tests are usually carried out on respiratory samples obtained through nasopharyngeal swabs; however, nasal swipe or sputum samples can also be used.
In general, the results are available within a few hours to two days.
Blood testing can be used, but it requires two blood samples taken with a distance of two weeks so that the results have a small time value.
Chinese scientists are able to insulate these coronavirus vulnerabilities and publish their genetic sequences so that laboratories around the world can independently develop polymerase (PCR (PCR) reaction testing to detect infections by this virus.
On April 4, 2020, antiviral tests (which can detect an active infection and if a person has previously been infected) is in development, but it has not been widely used.
China's experience with such testing shows its accuracy is only 60 to 70%.
The FDA in the United States approved the first point-of-care testing on March 21, 2020 for use at the end of that month. The diagnosis guidelines released by Zhongnan Hospital of the University of Wuhan suggested a method to detect infections based on a clinical picture and epidemiological risk.
Bilateral multilobar ground-glass opasity with peripheral, asymmetric, and posterior distribution is widely encountered at the beginning of an infection.
Subplaural domination, crazy paving (lobular setemplative thickening with varied alveolar replenishment), and consolidation can appear as the disease progresses.
Slight data is available on microscopic lesions and COVID-19 patophysiology.
The main pathological findings in the autopsy are:
Macroscopy: pleurisy, pericarditis, pulmonary consolidation, and pulmonary edema
Four types of severity of viral pneumonia can be observed:
Mild pulmonary edema: pulmonary edema, pneumocytic hyperplasia, large atypile pneumocytes, interstitial inflammatory with lemphocytic infiltration, and the formation of many-core gigantic cells
Severe pneed pneumonia: diffuse al-veolar (DAD) malfunction with diffuse al-veolar exudates.
DAD is the cause of acute prostitution syndrome (ARDS) and strenuous hypoxemia.
pneumonia in the healing stage: exudate arrangement in the alveolar cavity and pulmonary interstitial fibrosis
Blood: intravascular coagulation diminata (DIC); leukoerithroblastic reaction
The preventive measures to reduce the chances of infection include staying at home, avoiding crowded places, often washing hands with soap and water for at least 20 seconds, practicing good breathing hygiene, and avoiding touching the eyes, nose, or mouth with unwashed hands.
The CDC recommends covering the mouth and nose with a tissue when coughing or sneezing and recommends the use of an elbow inside if a tissue is unavailable.
Appropriate higiene hands after coughing or sneezing is recommended.
The CDC recommends the use of face coverings from fabrics in public places, in part to limit contagion by asymptomatic people. Social distancing strategies aim to reduce the contact of infected people with many people, by closing schools and workplaces, restricting trips, and canceling large-scale meetings in public places.
Restriction guidelines also include people maintaining a distance of at least 6 feet or 1.8 meters.
Because vaccines are anticipated not until the beginning of 2021, the main part in dealing with COVID-19 is to try to reduce the peak of the epidemic, known as "eflating the curve".
The CDC also recommends that people often wash their hands with soap and water for at least 20 seconds, especially after from the toilet or when the hands look dirty, before eating, as well as after exhaling the nose, coughing, or sneezing.
Further the CDC also recommends using alcohol-based hand sanitizer that contains at least 60% alcohol, but only when soap and water are not available. In areas where commercial hand sanitizer is unavailable, WHO provides two formulations for local production.
In this formulation, antimicrobial activity is derived from ethanol or isopropan.
Hydrogen peroxide is used to help eliminate bacterial spores in alcohol; this substance is "not an activated substance for hand antisepsis".
Glyserol is added as humektan.
People are treated with support treatments, which can include fluid therapy, oxygen support, and supporting other affected vital organs.
The CDC recommends people who suspect they're carrying the virus in order to wear a simple face mask.
Oxygenation membrane extractorporeum (ECMO) has been used to address the problem of respiratory failure, but the benefits are still being considered.
Hygiene and lifestyle and a healthy diet are recommended to increase immunity.
Supportive treatment may be useful in people who have mild symptoms in the early stages of the infection. WHO and China's National Health Commission have published recommendations to deal with hospital-preserved COVID-19 sufferers.
Intensive care experts and pulmonary experts in the U.S. have compiled treatment recommendations from various institutions into free resources, namely IBCC.
Until April 2020, there was no specific treatment for COVID-19.
For the symptoms, some medical personnel recommend paracetamol (acetaminophen) than ibuprofen for first-line use.
Precautionary measures must be taken to minimise the risk of transmission of the virus, especially in the health care order when performing procedures that can produce aerosols, such as intubation or hand ventilation.
For health workers caring for COVID-19 suffer sufferers, the CDC recommends placing the person in the Isolation Room of T-Air Infectious Infection (AIIR) in addition to using standard precautions, contact preventive measures, and cellular-air precautions. The CDC outlines guidelines for the use of personal protective equipment (APD) during the pandemic.
Recommendinary appliances are: APD cloak, respirator or face mask, eye protection, and medical glove. If it is available, respirators (not face masks) are better.
Respirator N95 is approved for the industrial order, but the FDA has authorited these masks for use based on EmerSIBurse Authorization (EUA).
Respirator N95 is designed to protect against airborne congenital particles such as dust, but the effectiveness against certain biological agents isn't guaranteed for unneathcounted use on the label.
If a mask isn't available, the CDC recommends using a face protector or, as a last-frolict attempt, a homemade mask.
Most COVID-19 cases don't get too heavy until they require mechanical or alternative ventilation, but in some cases it's heavy.
This type of respiratory support for people experiencing COVID-19-provided respiratory failure is being actively scrutinized for inpatient, and there is evidence that intubation can be avoided with high flow nasal cannula or bipositive air purse.
It is unavailable to whether any of these two methods produce the same benefits for people who are critically ill.
Some clinicians prefer to still use invasive mechanical ventilation, when available, because this technique limits the spread of aerosol particles compared to high-distance nasal kanculus. More severe cases occur in the elderly (people over the age of 60 years, and especially people who are over the age of 80 years).
Many developed countries don't have adequate per capita hospital beds, which limit the capacity of the health system to deal with a sudden spike in the number of COVID-19 cases is severe enough to require hospitalization.
A study in China found that 5% of intensive care units, a 2.3% required mechanical ventilation support, and 1.4% died.
In China, about 30% of COVID-19 sufferers who were hospitalized eventually entered the ICU.
Mechanical ventilation is becoming more complicated as acute breathing syndrome (ARDS) occurs in COVID-19 and oxygenation is becoming increasingly difficult.
Ventilators who are able to perform high pressure control and PEEP methods are needed to maximize oxygen delivery while minimizing the risk of pulmonary pulmonary and pneumotor related ventilators.
High PEEPs may not be available on old ventilators.
Research on potential treatments began in January 2020 and several antiviral drugs are in clinical trials.
Remdesivir seems most promising.
Although the development of the new drug may take until 2021, some drugs that are being tested have been approved for other use or are already in advanced testing.
Antiviral drugs can be tried in people with severe illness.
WHO-recommended Volunteers take part in a trial of the effectiveness and safety of potential treatment. The FDA has temporarily authorised for convaluescent plasma as an experimental treatment in a person's life case is very or immediately endangered.
Convalescent plasma hasn't underwent the necessary clinical studies to prove its safety and effectiveness for the disease.
In February 2020, China rolled out a mobile application to deal with this disease outbreak.
Users are required to enter their name and ID number.
This application is able to detect 'near contact' using Surveillance data so that it can detect potential infection risks.
Each user can also check the status of the other three users.
If the potential risk is detected, the app not only recommends self-quarantine, but also notifies local health workers. The omniscient analysis on cell phone data, face-recognition technology, cell phone trajectorization, and artificial wield intelligence is used to track infected people and people in contact with them in South Korea, Taiwan, and Singapore.
As of March of 2020, the Israeli government allowed a security agency to track the mobile data of people suspected of contaminating the coronavirus.
The move was taken to impose quarantine and protect people who may be in contact with infected citizens.
In addition, in March of 2020, Deutsche Telekom distributed an aggravation of telephone location data to Germany's federal government agency Robert Koch Institute in order to research and prevent the spread of the virus.
Russia used facial recognition technology to detect quarantine violators.
Italy's regional health commissioner Giulio Gallera, said he was told the mobile phone operator that "40% of citizens still continue to move places".
The German government conducted a 48-hour weekend hackathon with more than 42,000 attendees.
In addition, the President of Estonia, Kersti Kaljulaid, made a global call for creative solutions against the spread of the coronavirus.
People can experience stress due to quarantine, travel restrictions, side effects of the treatment, or the fear of the infection itself.
"Increasing social isolation, loneliness, health emergencies, health emergencies, stress and economic deterioration is a perfect storm that jeopardizes that jeopardizes people's mental and well-being."
This disease can occur lightly with little or asymptomatic, resembling other common upper airway diseases, such as colds.
Mild cases usually resolve within two weeks, while those suffering from severe or critical illness may take three to six weeks to recover.
Pregnant women may have a higher risk for severe COVID-19 infections based on data from similar viruses, such as SARS and MERS, but the data for COVID-19 is still lacking. In some people, COVID-19 can affect the lungs causing pneumonia.
In the worst-affected people, COVID-19 can quickly develop into an acute respiratory syndrome (ARDS) that leads to respiratory failure, septic shock, or maternity organ failure.
Complications related to COVID-19 include sepsis, abnormal blood clots, and damage to the heart, kidneys, and the liver.
Abnormalities of blood clots, in particular an increase in prothrombin time, are observed in 6% of hospitalized COVID-19 patients, while kidney function abnormalities are seen in 4% of this group.
About 20-30% of people with COVID-19 images show an increase in liver (transaminase enzymes).
According to the same report, the median time between the onslaught of symptoms and death is ten days, with five days spent in inpatients.
However, patients who are transferred to the ICU have a seven-day medianing time between hospitalization and death.
In a study that examined the initial cases, median times ranging from preliminary symptoms to death were 14 days, with a full range of six to 41 days.
In a study by China's National Health (NHC) Commission, men had a 2.8% death toll, while women had a mortality rate of 1.7%.
Histopathological examination of postmortem pulmonary pulmonary samples showed alveolar diffus damage with cellular fibromyxoid exudates in both lungs.
Viral cypathic changes are observed in pneumocytes.
Pulmonary images resembling acute proficient syndrome (ARDS).
At 11.8% of deaths reported by China's National Health Commission, heart damage was observed, with an increase in troponin or cardiac arrest.
According according to March data from the United States, 89% of inpatient people have congenital conditions. The availability of medical and socio-economic resources of an area can also affect mortality.
Estimated Mortality of the conditions varies due to differences in the region, but also due to methodological difficulties.
Lack of minor case calculations can cause death rates to be calculated too high.
However, the fact that death is the result of cases that have occurred in the past can mean the current Mortality rate is calculated too low.
Smokers are 1.4 times more likely to experience severe COVID-19 symptoms and about 2.4 times more likely to need intensive care or die compared to non-smokers. There are concerns about long-term sequela this disease.
Hong Kong's Hospital Authority found a 20% to 30% reduction in lung capacity in some people recovering from the disease, and pulmonary scans showed organ damage.
It can also cause post-intensive post-treatment syndrome after it resolves.
Until March 2020, it is not yet known if the previous infection provided an effective and long-term immunity in people recovering from this disease.
Immunity is seen as likely to be acquired, if looking at the behavior of other coronaviruses, but there has been reported cases cured of COVID-19 followed by a positive coronavirus test a few days thereafter.
This case is believed to be a deteriorating residual infection, and not a re-infection.
This virus is thought to be natural and derived from the animal, through a spreading infection.
As long as it's actually unknown, but as of December of 2019, the dispersal of infection is almost entirely driven by human-to-human contagion.
A study of the first 41 COVID-19-confirmed confirmed cases, published in January 2020 in The Lancet, revealed the earliest symptom-requisite date was December 1, 2019.
An official publication from the WHO reported the earliest symptoms on December 8, 2019.
In general, some measurements are used to calculate Mortality.
This figure varies by region and over time, as well as being influenced by the amount of testing, the quality of the health care system, handling options, time since the first outbreak, and the character of the population such as age, gender, and overall health.
At the end of 2019, the WHO established an emergency ICD-10 disease code U07.1 for laboratory-confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infections and U07.
According to Johns Hopkins University statistics, the ratio of deaths to cases globally is 6.9% (153,822/2,240.191) as of April 17, 2020.
The number varies by region. Other measurements include case fatality (CFR), which reflects the percentage of diagnosed people who die from certain diseases, and infection fatality (IFR), which reflects the percentage of infected (diagnosed and undiagnosed) people who die from certain diseases.
These stats aren't time-bound and follow a specific population ranging from infection to case resolution.
Although not everyone who is infected makes antibodies, the presence of antibodies can provide information about the number of people who have been infected.
In the epicenter of the outbreak in Italy, Castiglione d'Addda, a small village containing 4600 residents, 80 (1.7%) is dead.
In Gangellet, the disease is propagated through the Carnival festival, which spreads to young people, causing relatively lower mortality, and the possibility of not all COVID-19 deaths are classified as COVID-19 deaths.
Furthermore, the German health system has not been overwhelmed.
In the Netherlands, approximately 3% of the population may have antibodies, as reviewed from blood donations.
69 people (0.004% of the population) were conspired to die due to COVID-19.
Pandemic impacts and mortality rates are different for men and women.
Mortality is higher in men in studies conducted in China and Italy.
The highest risk for men is in their 50s, with the risk difference between new men and women is not apparent at the age of 90.
In China, the death toll for men amounted to 2.8 per cent and for women at 1.7 per cent.
The definite reason for differences based on this gender is unknown, but genetic and behavioural factors may be the reason.
Immunological differences based on sex, lower prevalence of smoking in women, and men who have comorbid conditions, such as hypertension, at a younger age than women can contribute to higher mortality in men.
In Europe, 57% of infected people were men and 72% of people who died from COVID-19 were men.
Until April 2020, the U.S. government did not track gender-related data for COVID-19 infections.
Research shows that viral diseases, such as Ebola, HIV, influenza, and SARS affect men and women differently.
Percentages of health workers who are women are taller, especially nurses, and they have a higher chance of being exposed to the virus.
The World Health Organization announced on February 11, 2020 that the disease's official name was "COVID-19".
WHO leader Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyes explained that CO is an abbreviation for coronavirus, VI for the virus, D for the disease, and 19 for when the outbreak was first identified: December 31, 2019.
The name was chosen to circumvent references to certain geographical locations (e.g. China), animal species, or a group of people, in line with international recommendations for naming aimed at preventing stigmatization. The virus that causes COVID-19 is named coronavirus severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
WHO is also using the "COVID-19 virus" and the "fabrile responsible for COVID-19" in public communication.
Both diseases and viruses are commonly referred to as “coronavirus”.
At the beginning of the outbreak in Wuhan, China, the virus and the disease are commonly referred to as "coronaviruses" and "TRAIN coronaviruses".
In January of 2020, WHO recommended 2019-nCoV 2019-nCov and acute respiratory illness 2019-nCov as a provisional name for viruses and diseases in accordance with 2015 guidelines that appealed not to use location names in disease and viral names.
The official COVID-19 and SARS-CoV-2 names were issued on February 11, 2020.
Due to capacity limitations in the standard supply chain, some digital manufacturers print health care materials such as nasal swipes and ventilator components.
In one instance, when an Italian hospital desperately needs a ventilator valve, and the supplier is unable to deliver on the required time scale, a local company is fabricated over and prints 100 necessary valves overnight.
After the beginning of the COVID-19 outbreak, conspiracy theories, misinformation, and disinformation sprawl resurfacing the origin, scale, prevention, treatment, and other aspects of the disease, are spreading rapidly online.
Humans appear to be able to spread the virus to several other animals.
Studies fail to find evidence of viral replication in pigs, ducks, and chickens.
There is no drug or vaccine that has not been approved to treat the disease.
An international research on COVID-19 vaccines and drugs is being carried out by government organizations, academic groups, and industry researchers.
In March, the World Health Organization initiated “SOLIDARITY Trial” to assess the effects of treating four existing antiviral contaminants with the most promulgated efficacy.
Vaccines aren't yet available, but various agencies are currently actively developing vaccinic candidates.
Previous research on SARS-CoV is used because SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 both use ACE2 receptors to enter human cells.
There are three vaccinated strategies that are being researched.
First, the researcher aims to create a whole virus vaccine.
The use of the virus, either inactivated or dead viruses, aims to quickly trigger the human body's immune response to new COVID-19 infections.
The second strategy, the subunit vaccine, aims to create a vaccine that makes the immune system sensitive to certain subunits in the virus.
In SARS-CoV-2 case, the study focused on S-spike proteins that help the virus infiltrate ACE2 enzyme receptors.
The third strategy is nucleic acid vaccination (DNA vaccine or RNA, a new technique for making vaccinations).
An experimental vaccine created with these strategies must be tested to find out its safety and efficacy. On March 16, 2020 the first clinical trial of a vaccine started with four volunteers in Seattle.
Vaccines contain an unhazardous genetic code copied from viruses that cause the disease. Antiboy-dependent increase has been suggested as a potential challenge for the development of the SARS-COV-2 vaccine, but this method is still controversial.
There are more than 300 active clinical trials taking place in April 2020.
The seven trials were evaluated an aforementioned treatment for malaria, including four studies against hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine.
An antiviral remedial drug given a new purpose formed most of China's research, with nine Phase III trials on remdesivir in several countries with a reporting deadline at the end of April.
As of April of 2020, a dynamic review of clinical development for COVID-19 vaccine and drug candidates already exists.Some existing antiviral drugs are being evaluated for the treatment of COVID-19, which includes remdesivir, chloroquine and hydroxiklocuin, lopinavir/ritonavir, and lopinavir/ritonavir combined with interferon beta.
In March of 2020, there's provisional evidence for redesivir efficacy.
Clinical improvement is observed in patients who are treated with remdesivir in emergency treatment.
Phase III clinical trials are being carried out in U.S., China, and Italy. Chlorokuin, previously used to treat malaria, was researched in China in February 2020, with preliminary results.
However, there was a call for a colleague's review of the research.
Korean and Chinese health authorities recommend the use of chloroquine.
However, the Wuhan Virology Institute, although it recommends a one-gram daily dose, notes that the dossage doubles is very dangerous and can be lethal.
On March 28, 2020, the FDA issued an emergency use authorization for hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine on the policy of doctors treating COVID-19 sufferers.The 7th-guesn-education also included interferon, ribavirin, or umifenovir for use against COVID-19.
Inititial data shows that high doses of ribavirin are needed to inhibit SARS-CoV-2 vitro.
Nitazoksanide has been recommended for further in vivo studies after showing SARS-CoV-2 inhibition in low concentrations.The study showed that early priming against bulge proteins by transmembrant protease 2 (TMPRSS2) is essential for the influgration of SARS-CoV-2 through interactions with ACE2 receptors.
The studies on chloroquine and hydroxychlorochuins with or without azithromycin have great limitations that have prevented the medical community from using these therapies without further research. Oseltamivir doesn't ravage SARS-CoV-2 in vitro and has no known role in COVID-19 treatment.
Communication cytokine storms can occur in an advanced severe COVID-19.
There is evidence that hydroxychlorokuins may have antisitokin properties. Tocilizumab has been included in the treatment guidelines by the Chinese National Health Commission after a small study was completed.
This drug is undergoing a non-random phase 2 test at the national level in Italy after showing positive results in people with severe illness.
Combined with serum ferritin blood tests to identify cytokine storms, this drug is intended to fight cytokine storms, which is thought to be the cause of death in some affected people.
In 2017, interleukin-6 receptor antagonists were approved by the FDA based on a retrospective case study for the treatment of cytokine release syndrome of a steroid-induced refraker by a different cause, CAR T cell therapy.
Until now, there has been no random and controlled evidence showing that tocilizumab is an efficacious treatment for CRS.
Defined and concentrated anti-ibody transfers produced by the system of people who have recovered from COVID-19 to people who need it is being researched as non-vaccine passive immunization methods.
This strategy is tried for SARS with uncluclusive results.
Neutralization of the virus is an expected working mechanism for passive antibody therapy can mediate a defense against SARS-CoV-2.
However, other mechanisms, such as cellular cytotoxicity that rely on antibodies and/or phagocytosis, are also possible.
Other forms of passive antibody therapy, for example, using monoclonal antibodies produced, are in development.
The production of convalescent serum, which in the form of fluid part of the patient's blood that has been cured and contains specific antibodies to this virus, can be increased for faster use.
